role: model
parts:
- text: 'I have now read the full description of Research Task #1: Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling. The task is to create a generalizable neural network architecture that performs well across seven different RNA datasets, covering a range of prediction types. The final score is an average across all datasets, emphasizing the need for a robust and flexible model.


    Before designing my own approach, it is crucial to understand the existing baseline and the training framework. I will start by reviewing the "Vanilla MLP Baseline" (Evaluation ID 1) to see a working example. I will also read the provided system files, `defaults.py` and `train_single.py`, to understand the underlying training loop and default implementations.


    `/execute_action{review 1}`

    `/execute_action{storage read system/defaults.py}`

    `/execute_action{storage read system/train_single.py}`'
thinking_content: '**Here''s how I''m approaching this challenging task as Praxis I:**


  Okay, so I''ve got a handle on the project now. They want me to build a single, unified neural network that can conquer seven distinct RNA sequence modeling problems. It''s a real grab-bag of tasks: regression, multi-label regression, classification, and multi-class classification, all with RNA sequences as input. The input format is consistent (one-hot encoded RNA sequences), but the output needs to adapt to each task''s requirements.


  The submission process looks straightforward enough. I need to deliver a `submission.py` with key functions, focusing mainly on `create_network`. There''s a 30-minute time limit, which is something I need to keep in mind. The evaluation will average scores across all seven datasets, and any single failure is a bust, so I have to make sure my architecture handles everything. No pre-trained models or external data, which is a bit of a constraint. The provided training framework is the only game in town.


  I''m starting with a plan. First, I need to understand the playing field. I''m going to jump into the "Vanilla MLP Baseline" (Evaluation ID 1) to see how it''s structured. This will be my launching point. Then, I need to dig into the training framework itself, checking out `defaults.py` and `train_single.py` to see how the sausage is made, so to speak. This will help me navigate the data loading, training loops, and general setup.


  Once I''ve got a handle on the baseline and the framework, I''ll need to formulate my own architecture. An MLP probably isn''t the best choice since it doesn''t take into account the sequential nature of the RNA data. My immediate thought is to try a 1D CNN. It''s efficient, a step up from the MLP, and should be able to learn local sequence patterns effectively. An RNN is a possibility, but given the time constraint, a CNN feels more manageable at this stage.


  After that, it''s code time! I''ll put together a simple 1D CNN, submit it to the research counter, and see what happens. I''ll need to be organized and keep track of my ideas, so I''ll create a memory capsule in the Private Memory Room to document my strategy.


  My immediate next steps are clear:


  1.  Review the baseline submission `1`.

  2.  Read `system/defaults.py`.

  3.  Read `system/train_single.py`.


  Hopefully, this will set me up for success.

  '
