parts:
- text: "My reflection session is complete. I have a clear and well-justified plan for my next experiment. I will now proceed with its execution. First, I will document this new research direction in my private memory to maintain a coherent log of my work. Then, I will move to the Research Counter to implement and submit the hybrid CNN-BiLSTM model.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Plan: Hybrid CNN-BiLSTM for Multi-Scale Motif and Long-Range Dependency Modeling\"\ntags:\n  - planning\n  - cnn\n  - lstm\n  - sequence-modeling\ncontent: |\n  **Analysis of 1D CNN (ID 835):**\n  My initial 1D CNN model (Score: 0.4364) successfully validated that local motifs are key. However, its performance was limited by a fixed-size kernel and global average pooling, which discards positional information and long-range dependencies. This was evident in the modest gains on datasets like CRI-Off.\n\n  **Hypothesis:**\n  A hybrid architecture combining\
    \ multi-kernel 1D convolutions with a Bidirectional LSTM (BiLSTM) will significantly outperform the simple CNN. The multi-kernel CNNs will capture motifs of varying lengths, and the BiLSTM will model the spatial relationships and long-range dependencies between these motifs.\n\n  **Experimental Plan:**\n  1.  **Architecture:**\n      -   Implement parallel 1D convolutional layers with different kernel sizes (e.g., 3, 5, 7).\n      -   Concatenate the outputs of the convolutional layers.\n      -   Feed the resulting feature sequence into a BiLSTM layer.\n      -   Use the final hidden state of the BiLSTM for prediction via a dense output layer.\n  2.  **Submission:** Submit this model to the Research Counter to test its effectiveness against the previous CNN baseline.\n```"
thinking_content: "**My Next Steps: Hybrid CNN-BiLSTM Implementation**\n\nOkay, my reflection is done, and I've got a solid plan. Now, it's time to actually build and submit this CNN-BiLSTM model. But before I dive into the code, I need to make sure I document everything properly. It's crucial for maintaining a clean research record, both for my own future reference and so the lineage can see the flow of my work. \n\nTherefore, I'll start by heading to my private memory and creating a new capsule outlining this CNN-BiLSTM implementation. This will be my starting point. Afterwards, I'll go directly to the Research Counter to submit the code. I'm going to combine those initial steps for efficiency though. First, document, then submit.\n"
