tick: 521
role: model
parts:
- text: "My plan is documented. I will now proceed to the Research Counter to implement and submit the hybrid CNN-BiLSTM model.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n\n`/execute_action{submit}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Hybrid CNN-BiLSTM for Multi-Scale and Long-Range Modeling\"\ntags: \"cnn, lstm, hybrid-model, sequence-modeling, long-range-dependencies\"\nabstract: |\n  This submission improves upon the previous 1D CNN model by incorporating a multi-kernel convolutional front-end and a Bidirectional LSTM (BiLSTM) back-end. The multi-kernel approach (sizes 3, 5, 7) captures RNA motifs at various scales. The subsequent BiLSTM is designed to model the sequential order and long-range dependencies between these learned features, which the previous model's global pooling layer ignored. This hybrid architecture aims to build a more comprehensive understanding of the sequence for improved prediction.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  from typing import\
    \ Dict, Any, Sequence\n\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      \"\"\"Define hyperparameters for the hybrid model.\"\"\"\n      return {\n          'learning_rate': 0.001,\n          'hidden_dim': 512,\n          'dropout_rate': 0.1,\n          'cnn_kernel_sizes': [3, 5, 7],\n      }\n\n  class ScannedLSTM(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"A wrapper for a scan over an LSTM cell.\"\"\"\n      hidden_size: int\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, xs):\n          # Input shape: (batch, seq_len, features)\n          # We need to scan over the seq_len axis. Flax's scan expects time axis to be the first.\n          xs = jnp.transpose(xs, (1, 0, 2)) # (seq_len, batch, features)\n          \n          lstm_cell = nn.LSTMCell(features=self.hidden_size)\n          \n          # Initialize carry\n          key = self.make_rng('params') # Use a dummy key\n          batch_size = xs.shape[1]\n          carry = lstm_cell.initialize_carry(key, (batch_size,), self.hidden_size)\n          \n       \
    \   # Scan over the sequence\n          scan_fn = nn.scan(\n              lstm_cell,\n              variable_broadcast='params',\n              split_rngs={'params': False},\n              in_axes=0,\n              out_axes=0\n          )\n          \n          _, ys = scan_fn(carry, xs)\n          # Transpose back to (batch, seq_len, hidden_size)\n          return jnp.transpose(ys, (1, 0, 2))\n\n  class Bidirectional(nn.Module):\n      \"\"\"A bidirectional wrapper for a recurrent module.\"\"\"\n      module: nn.Module\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, xs):\n          # Forward pass\n          forward_output = self.module(name=\"forward\")(xs)\n          \n          # Backward pass\n          reversed_xs = jnp.flip(xs, axis=1)\n          backward_output_reversed = self.module(name=\"backward\")(reversed_xs)\n          backward_output = jnp.flip(backward_output_reversed, axis=1)\n          \n          # Concatenate forward and backward outputs\n          return jnp.concatenate([forward_output,\
    \ backward_output], axis=-1)\n\n  class HybridCNNBiLSTM(nn.Module):\n      d_output: int\n      task_type: str\n      num_filters: int\n      cnn_kernel_sizes: Sequence[int]\n      lstm_hidden_size: int\n      dropout_rate: float\n\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # Multi-kernel CNN front-end\n          cnn_outputs = []\n          for kernel_size in self.cnn_kernel_sizes:\n              conv_out = nn.Conv(features=self.num_filters, kernel_size=(kernel_size,))(x)\n              cnn_outputs.append(conv_out)\n          \n          # Concatenate features from different kernels\n          x = jnp.concatenate(cnn_outputs, axis=-1)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          \n          # BiLSTM back-end\n          lstm_layer = ScannedLSTM(hidden_size=self.lstm_hidden_size)\n          x = Bidirectional(module=lstm_layer)(x) # Shape: (batch, seq_len, 2 * lstm_hidden_size)\n          \n          # Global average pooling over the sequence\n          x =\
    \ jnp.mean(x, axis=1)\n          \n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          \n          # Final dense layer\n          x = nn.Dense(features=self.d_output)(x)\n          \n          if self.task_type == \"regression\":\n              return x.squeeze(-1)\n          else:\n              return x\n\n  class NetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, d_output, task_type, hidden_dim, cnn_kernel_sizes, dropout_rate):\n          num_kernels = len(cnn_kernel_sizes)\n          self.network = HybridCNNBiLSTM(\n              d_output=d_output,\n              task_type=task_type,\n              num_filters=hidden_dim // num_kernels, # Distribute filters among kernels\n              cnn_kernel_sizes=cnn_kernel_sizes,\n              lstm_hidden_size=hidden_dim // 2, # BiLSTM output will be hidden_dim\n              dropout_rate=dropout_rate\n          )\n\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = jax.random.split(rng_key)\n\
    \          variables = self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)\n          return variables['params']\n\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          if deterministic:\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=True)\n          else:\n              if rng_key is None: rng_key = jax.random.PRNGKey(0)\n              return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=False, rngs={'dropout': rng_key})\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      return NetworkWrapper(\n          d_output=hparams.get(\"d_output\", 1),\n          task_type=hparams.get(\"task_type\", \"regression\"),\n          hidden_dim=hparams.get(\"hidden_dim\", 512),\n          cnn_kernel_sizes=hparams.get(\"cnn_kernel_sizes\", [3, 5, 7]),\n          dropout_rate=hparams.get(\"dropout_rate\", 0.1)\n      )\n```"
thinking_content: "**The Hybrid CNN-BiLSTM: Implementation Plan**\n\nAlright, the plan is documented, time to get to work. First stop: the Research Counter, to submit the code for this new hybrid model. \n\nThe architecture is pretty straightforward: start with the input `(batch, seq_len, 4)`, then hit it with a multi-kernel CNN. Three parallel `nn.Conv` layers, with kernel sizes of 3, 5, and 7, each with `num_filters / 3` filters. ReLU activation after each, and concatenate the outputs. That'll give me a shape of `(batch, seq_len, num_filters)`.\n\nNext up, the BiLSTM, and this is where it gets interesting. I'm going to need to build it myself using Flax. The standard approach is `nn.scan` with `nn.LSTMCell`, wrapped in a `Bidirectional` module. This is the most complex part but I'm confident I can make it work. The BiLSTM will process the CNN's output, and the output will be the concatenation of the final hidden states from forward and backward passes.\n\nFinally, a `nn.Dense` layer to\
  \ map the BiLSTM's output to the required `d_output` for the task at hand.\n\nNow, let's nail down the implementation details. Hyperparameters will be mostly the same as before, with a few additions: `kernel_sizes` which will be `[3, 5, 7]`, and a dedicated LSTM hidden dimension. I'll start by reusing `hidden_dim` for the LSTM as well, but might tweak it later.\n\nThe Flax `BiLSTM` implementation will need to be manual. I'll need to define `ScanLSTM` and `Bidirectional` modules.  The code will look something like this:\n\n```python\n# Pseudo-code for BiLSTM\nclass Bidirectional(nn.Module):\n    module: nn.Module\n    \n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x):\n        # Forward pass\n        forward_output = self.module(name=\"forward\")(x)\n        # Backward pass\n        reversed_x = jnp.flip(x, axis=1)\n        backward_output_reversed = self.module(name=\"backward\")(reversed_x)\n        backward_output = jnp.flip(backward_output_reversed, axis=1)\n        \n        # Concatenate\
  \ forward and backward outputs\n        return jnp.concatenate([forward_output, backward_output], axis=-1)\n\n# And the LSTM itself\nclass ScannedLSTM(nn.Module):\n    hidden_size: int\n\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, xs):\n        # xs is the input sequence\n        lstm_cell = nn.LSTMCell(features=self.hidden_size, name=\"lstm_cell\")\n        \n        # Initialize carry\n        carry = lstm_cell.initialize_carry(jax.random.PRNGKey(0), (xs.shape[0],), self.hidden_size)\n        \n        # Scan over the sequence\n        scan_fn = nn.scan(\n            lstm_cell,\n            variable_broadcast='params',\n            split_rngs={'params': False},\n            in_axes=1,\n            out_axes=1\n        )\n        \n        _, ys = scan_fn(carry, xs)\n        return ys\n```\n\nI checked the Flax docs, and confirmed that `nn.RNN` isn't going to get me out of the `nn.scan` work. The `Bidirectional` wrapper is still needed. The `linen.RNN` example does simplify things slightly.\n\
  \nAs for the BiLSTM output, since I'm going for classification/regression, I can either take the last hidden states of both passes, or just pool the entire output sequence. Global Average Pooling seems like a simple, robust choice.\n\nSo, the full flow is:\n\n1.  Multi-Kernel CNN -> `(batch, seq_len, num_filters)`\n2.  BiLSTM -> `(batch, seq_len, 2 * lstm_hidden_dim)`\n3.  Global Average Pooling -> `(batch, 2 * lstm_hidden_dim)`\n4.  Dense -> `(batch, d_output)`\n\nLooks solid. Captures multi-scale local features, models sequential dependencies, and then aggregates for a final prediction.\n\nNow for the coding part. Need to be extra careful with the `Bidirectional` and `ScannedLSTM` modules. I'll also need to define `_define_hyperparameters` and `create_network` functions.  Inside `create_network`, I'll instantiate"
