- text: "My reflection on the failed Transformer experiment is complete. I have a new plan that is more grounded in the evidence from my previous submissions. The next step is to add an attention mechanism to my best-performing CNN-BiLSTM model.\n\nFirst, I will document this new plan in my private memory to maintain a clear research log. Then, I will proceed to the Research Counter to implement it.\n\n`/execute_action{goto private_memory}`\n\n`/execute_action{create}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Plan: Augmenting CNN-BiLSTM with an Attention Mechanism\"\ntags:\n  - planning\n  - attention\n  - lstm\n  - cnn\n  - cri-off\ncontent: |\n  **Analysis of Transformer Failure (ID 851):**\n  The CNN-Transformer model (Score: 0.4967) was a significant step backward. This indicates that the strong sequential inductive bias of the BiLSTM is critical for most tasks. The Transformer's flexibility was a detriment, likely due to data limitations and the directional nature of the biological signals.\n\n  **Revisiting\
    \ the CRI-Off Anomaly:**\n  The simplest model (1D CNN) performed best on CRI-Off, suggesting that the signal is local and sparse, and that the global context captured by more complex models acts as noise.\n\n  **Hypothesis:**\n  Replacing the simple mean pooling in the CNN-BiLSTM architecture with a learned attention mechanism will improve performance, particularly on CRI-Off. The attention layer will allow the model to learn to assign higher importance to the sparse, relevant features and down-weight the noisy, irrelevant context present in the BiLSTM's output. This should improve CRI-Off performance without sacrificing the high scores on other datasets.\n\n  **Experimental Plan:**\n  1.  **Architecture:** Start with the validated CNN-BiLSTM architecture (ID 843).\n  2.  **Modification:** Replace the `jnp.mean` operation with an additive attention layer that computes a weighted average of the BiLSTM's hidden states.\n  3.  **Submission:** Submit this new model to test the hypothesis.\n\
    ```"
