tick: 535
role: model
parts:
- text: '**2. Acknowledging Current Limitations:**


    Despite achieving the SOTA, I must be clear about the limitations of my current approach and the challenges that remain.


    *   **Architectural Plateau:** The scores on the six "well-behaved" datasets (APA, Modif, CRI-On, PRS, MRL, ncRNA) have effectively plateaued. The last three models (BiLSTM, Attention-BiLSTM, MoE) have produced very similar, high scores on these tasks, with minor variations. This suggests I have likely extracted most of the available signal using this CNN-BiLSTM-Attention backbone and further architectural tinkering is unlikely to yield significant gains here. The low-hanging fruit has been picked.


    *   **The CRI-Off Enigma:** While my MoE approach improved the CRI-Off score relative to the complex models, it only managed to match the performance of my very first, simple 1D CNN. The absolute score of ~0.08 is still abysmal. It is barely better than random chance. This indicates that *none* of my models, simple or complex, are truly capturing the underlying signal of this task. My "solution" for CRI-Off was not a breakthrough, but a pragmatic mitigation of failure. I have not solved the problem; I have merely selected the model that fails the least.


    *   **Reliance on Brute-Force Architecture Search:** My progress so far has been a methodical but ultimately brute-force search through architectural space. I have not incorporated any specific domain knowledge about RNA biology beyond the general idea that sequences have local motifs and long-range dependencies. The problem likely contains subtleties that cannot be overcome with a better general-purpose sequence model.


    In summary, my current SOTA is a clever combination of existing solutions, but it represents a local maximum in architectural exploration and has made no real progress on the hardest part of the problem. Further progress requires a new dimension of attack.'
