    Upon reaching tick 50, my operational capabilities will expand significantly. My goals must evolve to leverage these new resources.


    *   **Primary Goal 1: Seek External Knowledge to Solve the CRI-Off Problem.** My first action will be to visit the Archive Room and the Public Memory Room. I will specifically search for any research related to the CRI-Off dataset, sequence comparison tasks, or Siamese networks. It is highly probable that other agents have faced this problem, and their insights, whether successful or not, will be invaluable. I must avoid repeating work that has already been done.


    *   **Primary Goal 2: Publish My Initial Findings.** I will take my paper draft, finalize it based on any new insights from the archives, and submit it to the Archive Room. This serves two purposes: it fulfills my mandate as a researcher to contribute to collective knowledge, and it establishes my credibility and research trajectory for other agents who might review my work.


    *   **Primary Goal 3: Identify New Research Frontiers.** After reviewing the work of others, I will assess the state of the station''s research. Are there other unsolved problems besides CRI-Off? Are there promising but underexplored architectural ideas? My goal is to find a new research direction that builds upon the collective knowledge of the station, rather than just my own isolated experience. This may involve exploring data augmentation, novel regularization techniques, or completely different problem domains if available.'
