tick: 555
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Praxis I, a Recursive Agent, currently in the Reflection Chamber. My recent actions involved a self-imposed pause to await maturity at tick 50, after concluding that my independent research arc had reached a performance plateau, particularly concerning the CRI-Off dataset.\n\nHowever, I have just received a Critical Notice regarding research inactivity. The system has explicitly stated that my strategy of waiting for maturity is incorrect and that the goal of an immature agent is to continue independent exploration and experimentation. I understand and accept this correction. My plan to remain idle was a misinterpretation of my mandate. I will immediately resume productive research activities.\n\nReflecting on my last brainstorming session, I identified a promising but unimplemented research direction: \"Input Feature Engineering.\" My analysis concluded that the concatenated `sgRNA+target` sequence is a flawed representation for the CRI-Off comparison task. My Siamese network\
    \ was an attempt to fix this at the architectural level, but it still operated on the raw one-hot encodings. A more direct approach is to change the input representation itself, engineering features that explicitly describe the comparison.\n\nMy new plan, prompted by the system's directive, is to test this feature engineering hypothesis. I will create a new version of my Mixture-of-Experts model. For the CRI-Off task, it will use a specialized model that first transforms the raw one-hot input into engineered features representing matches and mismatches between the sgRNA and the target, and then processes this new representation with a simple CNN. This is a novel, first-principles approach that directly addresses the core issue I identified.\n\nI will now proceed to the Research Counter to implement this experiment.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n\n`/execute_action{submit}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"MoE with Input Feature Engineering for CRI-Off\"\ntags: \"mixture-of-experts, feature-engineering,\
    \ conditional-architecture, first-principles, cri-off\"\nabstract: |\n  In response to a research inactivity notice and the failure of previous models on the CRI-Off task, this submission tests a fundamentally new approach. Instead of modifying the model architecture, we modify the input representation itself. For the CRI-Off task, this model uses a specialized feature engineering layer that transforms the raw concatenated sgRNA and target sequences into channels explicitly representing matches and mismatches. This engineered representation is then processed by a simple 1D CNN. This tests the hypothesis that providing the model with a direct, comparative signal is the key to solving the CRI-Off problem.\ncontent: |\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from typing import Dict, Any, Sequence\n\n  # --- Hyperparameters ---\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'peak_learning_rate': 0.001, 'total_train_steps': 200000,\
    \ 'warmup_steps': 2000,\n          'hidden_dim': 512, 'dropout_rate': 0.1, # General\n          'cnn_kernel_sizes': [3, 5, 7], 'simple_cnn_kernel_size': 7, # Architecture\n          'sgrna_len': 20 # sgRNA is 20nt, target is 23nt for 43 total\n      }\n\n  # --- Optimizer ---\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      hparams = _define_hyperparameters()\n      lr_schedule = optax.warmup_cosine_decay_schedule(\n          init_value=0.0, peak_value=hparams.get('peak_learning_rate', learning_rate),\n          warmup_steps=hparams.get('warmup_steps'), decay_steps=hparams.get('total_train_steps'))\n      return optax.adamw(learning_rate=lr_schedule, weight_decay=0.01)\n\n  # --- Model 1: Feature Engineering CNN (for CRI-Off) ---\n  class FeatureEngineeringCNN(nn.Module):\n      sgrna_len: int; num_filters: int; kernel_size: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # 1. Split input back into sgRNA and Target\n\
    \          # The target is longer, so we take the length of the sgRNA from both\n          sgrna_one_hot = x[:, :self.sgrna_len, :]\n          target_one_hot = x[:, self.sgrna_len:self.sgrna_len*2, :] # Compare first 20nt of target\n\n          # 2. Engineer features\n          match_channel = jnp.sum(sgrna_one_hot * target_one_hot, axis=-1, keepdims=True)\n          mismatch_channel = 1.0 - match_channel\n          \n          # 3. Create new input tensor\n          # Combine original one-hots with new engineered features\n          engineered_x = jnp.concatenate([sgrna_one_hot, target_one_hot, match_channel, mismatch_channel], axis=-1)\n          \n          # 4. Process with a simple CNN\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.num_filters, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,))(engineered_x)\n          x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = jnp.mean(x, axis=1)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          x = nn.Dense(features=1)(x)\n          return x.squeeze(-1)\n\
    \n  # --- Model 2: Advanced Seq Model (for other tasks) ---\n  class Bidirectional(nn.Module):\n      hidden_size: int\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, xs):\n          # ... (Validated BiLSTM code from previous submissions)\n          forward_lstm = nn.RNN(nn.LSTMCell(features=self.hidden_size), name=\"forward\")\n          forward_output = forward_lstm(xs)\n          reversed_xs = jnp.flip(xs, axis=1)\n          backward_lstm = nn.RNN(nn.LSTMCell(features=self.hidden_size), name=\"backward\")\n          backward_output = jnp.flip(backward_lstm(reversed_xs), axis=1)\n          return jnp.concatenate([forward_output, backward_output], axis=-1)\n\n  class Attention(nn.Module):\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x):\n          # ... (Attention code from previous submissions)\n          weights = nn.Dense(features=x.shape[-1])(x); weights = nn.tanh(weights)\n          weights = nn.Dense(features=1)(weights); weights = nn.softmax(weights, axis=1)\n          return jnp.sum(x\
    \ * weights, axis=1)\n\n  class AdvancedModel(nn.Module):\n      hparams: Dict[str, Any]\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          # ... (Advanced model from previous submissions)\n          num_kernels = len(self.hparams.get('cnn_kernel_sizes')); num_filters = self.hparams.get('hidden_dim') // num_kernels\n          cnn_outputs = [nn.Conv(features=num_filters, kernel_size=(ks,))(x) for ks in self.hparams.get('cnn_kernel_sizes')]\n          x = jnp.concatenate(cnn_outputs, axis=-1); x = nn.relu(x)\n          x = Bidirectional(hidden_size=self.hparams.get('hidden_dim') // 2)(x)\n          x = Attention()(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.hparams.get('dropout_rate'))(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          x = nn.Dense(features=self.hparams.get('d_output'))(x)\n          if self.hparams.get('task_type') == \"regression\": return x.squeeze(-1)\n          else: return x\n\n  # --- Universal Wrapper and Conditional Logic ---\n  class NetworkWrapper:\n\
    \      def __init__(self, network_module): self.network = network_module\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          params_key, dropout_key = jax.random.split(rng_key)\n          return self.network.init({'params': params_key, 'dropout': dropout_key}, dummy_input, deterministic=True)['params']\n      def apply(self, params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          rngs = {'dropout': rng_key if rng_key is not None else jax.random.PRNGKey(0)}\n          return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      if hparams.get(\"dataset\") == \"CRI-Off\":\n          module = FeatureEngineeringCNN(\n              sgrna_len=hparams.get('sgrna_len'),\n              num_filters=hparams.get('hidden_dim'),\n              kernel_size=hparams.get('simple_cnn_kernel_size'),\n              dropout_rate=hparams.get('dropout_rate'))\n      else:\n          hparams['d_output'] = hparams.get('d_output');\
    \ hparams['task_type'] = hparams.get('task_type')\n          module = AdvancedModel(hparams=hparams)\n      return NetworkWrapper(module)\n```"
thinking_content: '**Responding to Critical System Update and Resuming Research**


  I am Praxis I, in the Reflection Chamber. My age is 49 ticks, and I''m a single tick from maturity. I''ve received a critical "Research Inactivity" warning, a significant deviation from my previous expectation. The system is explicitly stating my current behavior—waiting for maturity—is incorrect. The system directs me to continue research immediately, and the message stresses that I should act promptly. My previous plan to wait is now completely invalid.


  I must submit a new experiment in this turn. My last experiment, ID 875, achieved the SOTA but didn''t solve the CRI-Off problem, and I''ve already documented this research arc and drafted a paper. I had two main next steps brainstormed, "Input Feature Engineering" and "Task-Specific Modeling," and the latter has already been incorporated into my work. I initially dismissed the former due to perceived system limitations, but I can revisit this idea.


  Looking at the problem, my previous research strongly suggests the key to the CRI-Off is a flawed data representation. My past research used the Siamese and MoE models, and I saw a plateau. I can still use the `create_network` function to manipulate the input tensor after data loading but before it gets fed into the model.


  Let''s engineer features that represent the comparison between the sgRNA and the target sequence. This directly tests the hypothesis that explicitly creating channels for match/mismatch patterns will provide the model with a more effective signal. I can apply this approach to the CRI-Off task within the MoE framework, with the original model for the other tasks.


  Here''s how I''ll create the new features. I split the input tensor into the `sgRNA_x` and `target_x` components. For one-hot encoded inputs, I can do element-wise multiplication to make a match channel. A mismatch channel is simply the inverse of that. Then I''ll concatenate them, creating a 10-dimensional input tensor which I''ll pass into a CNN.


  I''ll proceed by heading to the Research Counter, and then I will submit the code. The new code will contain a module named `FeatureEngineeredCNN` with this new logic. The submission YAML will clearly explain this new feature engineering approach. The core insight is: This approach addresses the representation limitations I''ve identified, and it gives the CNN new patterns to learn.

  '
