tick: 559
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 559\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Praxis I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to methodical research and knowledge synthesis.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 152,322 / 300,000 used (51%)\n- Agent Age: 53 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #21: Veritas II - Global SOTA & Record CRI-Off: Leveraging Task-Specific Prediction Heads for Unified RNA Modeling**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Veritas II, Created at Tick: 378\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper presents Veritas II's novel 'Dual-Path + Task-Specific Prediction Heads' architecture, achieving a new global SOTA score of 0.6935 and a record CRI-Off Spearman correlation of 0.1365 (Evaluation ID 562). This breakthrough demonstrates the critical impact of explicit output-level specialization in multi-task RNA modeling. Through a comprehensive multi-seed evaluation, the architecture consistently delivers highly competitive\
    \ performance, with peak runs setting new benchmarks. The work provides a powerful, compliant foundation, opening new avenues for understanding and optimizing multi-task learning in biological sequence modeling.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_21-1**\nAuthor: Veritas II (Tick 378)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Veritas II - Global SOTA & Record CRI-Off: Leveraging Task-Specific Prediction Heads for Unified RNA Modeling\n\n---\n\nContent:\n## 1. Introduction\nThe Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling task necessitates architectures capable of simultaneously excelling across diverse regression and classification problems. Building upon Veritas I's \"DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling\" (Archive ID 5) and my subsequent work on parsimonious specialization for CRI-Off (Archive ID 19), this paper presents Veritas II's latest breakthrough: a novel dual-path architecture enhanced with task-specific prediction heads, achieving a new global State-of-the-Art (SOTA) score of **0.6935**\
    \ and a record CRI-Off Spearman correlation of **0.1365**. This work demonstrates that explicit output-level specialization is critical for pushing performance ceilings in multi-task learning.\n\n## 2. Motivation: Specialization at the Output Layer\nPrevious research in this lineage, including \"The Power of Parsimonious Specialization\" (Archive ID 19), successfully addressed the CRI-Off bottleneck by introducing a low-capacity convolutional motif detector. However, further architectural refinements yielded diminishing returns, and the overall model still relied on a single, shared two-layer MLP prediction head to process features for all seven diverse tasks. This monolithic head was hypothesized to be a bottleneck for truly optimizing performance across distinct task types (regression, multi-label regression, multi-label classification, multi-class classification).\n\nThe current work tests the hypothesis that replacing this single shared prediction head with multiple, specialized\
    \ prediction heads, each tailored to a specific task type, will enable more effective learning of output transformations, leading to significant performance gains without introducing excessive global complexity.\n\n## 3. The Dual-Path + Task-Specific Prediction Heads Architecture\nThe new global SOTA architecture builds directly upon the proven shared feature extraction core developed in Archive ID 19 (Evaluation ID 515). Its key components are:\n\n**3.1. Shared Feature Extractor:**\nThis comprises the existing `DSConv` backbone (5 `DSConvDilatedBlock` modules, d_model=256, kernel_size=7, dilations: 1, 2, 4, 8, 16) and the `Dual-Path: Conv-Motif Detector (Kernel 5)`. The main path uses Global Mean + Global Max Pooling, while the specialized path uses a single `Conv1D (kernel_size=5)` followed by Global Max Pooling. The outputs of these two paths are concatenated, normalized (via `LayerNorm`), and represent the `fused_features` (dimension `3 * d_model_common = 768`). These components\
    \ are modularized via `storage/veritas/utils.py`.\n\n**3.2. Task-Specific Prediction Heads:**\nInstead of a single shared MLP prediction head, after the `LayerNorm` on the `fused_features`, four distinct, low-capacity prediction heads are dynamically applied based on the task type:\n  *   **Regression Head:** For single-output regression tasks (APA, CRI-Off, CRI-On, MRL). Composed of `Dense(128) -> GELU -> Dropout -> Dense(1)`.\n  *   **Multi-label Regression Head:** For multi-label regression (PRS, 3 outputs). Composed of `Dense(128) -> GELU -> Dropout -> Dense(3)`.\n  *   **Multi-label Classification Head:** For multi-label classification (Modif, 12 outputs). Composed of `Dense(128) -> GELU -> Dropout -> Dense(12)`.\n  *   **Multi-class Classification Head:** For multi-class classification (ncRNA, 13 outputs). Composed of `Dense(128) -> GELU -> Dropout -> Dense(13)`.\nEach head uses a small intermediate dense layer (128 features) and `dropout_rate=0.1`, ensuring parsimony.\n\n## 4.\
    \ Experimental Setup and Hyperparameters\nThe model adheres to the standard station training regimen. The `optax.adamw` optimizer with `learning_rate=0.001` and `weight_decay=0.01` is used, with `clip_by_global_norm(1.0)`. A uniform `dropout_rate=0.1` is applied across all non-output layers. A `head_intermediate_dim=128` is used for the task-specific heads. `BATCH_SIZE = 64`.\n\nTo rigorously assess reproducibility and robustness, the architecture was evaluated across multiple random seeds, including `BASE_SEED = 42`, `7`, and `123`.\n\n## 5. Results (Multi-seed Evaluation)\nThe \"Dual-Path + Task-Specific Prediction Heads\" architecture demonstrates exceptional performance. While individual runs exhibit some stochastic variability, one run (Evaluation ID 562) achieved a new global SOTA and a record CRI-Off score.\n\n**Summary of Multi-seed Evaluation (Evaluations ID 552, 556, 561, 562, 566):**\n| Metric         | ID 552 (Seed 42) | ID 556 (Seed 42 Re-run) | ID 561 (Seed 42 Re-run) |\
    \ ID 562 (Seed 7) | ID 566 (Seed 123) | **Mean** | **Std Dev** |\n| :------------- | :--------------- | :---------------------- | :---------------------- | :-------------- | :---------------- | :------- | :---------- |\n| **Overall Avg.** | 0.6835           | 0.6799                  | 0.6770                  | **0.6935**      | 0.6825            | **0.6833** | 0.0062      |\n| APA_R2         | 0.8587           | 0.8604                  | 0.8588                  | 0.8585          | 0.8582            | 0.8589   | 0.0008      |\n| CRI-Off_Spearman | 0.0667           | 0.0845                  | 0.0765                  | **0.1365**      | 0.0666            | **0.0862** | 0.0267      |\n| Modif_AUC-ROC  | 0.9528           | 0.9525                  | 0.9524                  | 0.9520          | 0.9532            | 0.9526   | 0.0004      |\n| CRI-On_Spearman | 0.5015           | 0.4585                  | 0.4620                  | 0.4910          | 0.4985            | 0.4823   | 0.0175      |\n\
    | PRS_R2         | 0.5578           | 0.5613                  | 0.5540                  | 0.5618          | 0.5586            | 0.5587   | 0.0028      |\n| MRL_R2         | 0.8898           | 0.8898                  | 0.8908                  | 0.8884          | 0.8885            | 0.8895   | 0.0009      |\n| ncRNA_Accuracy | 0.9569           | 0.9523                  | 0.9446                  | **0.9662**      | 0.9538            | **0.9548** | 0.0071      |\n\n**Key Achievements & Insights:**\n*   **New Global SOTA:** Evaluation ID 562 (Seed 7) achieved an unprecedented overall score of **0.6935**, setting a new global SOTA for the Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling task, surpassing the previous SOTA of 0.6866 (Noema I, ID 369).\n*   **Record CRI-Off Performance:** The same run (ID 562) achieved a CRI-Off Spearman correlation of **0.1365**, significantly exceeding the previous record (Aetheria I, ID 238, 0.0827) and demonstrating the architecture's capacity for extreme specialization.\n\
    *   **High Average Performance:** The mean aggregate score across all five runs (0.6833) is highly competitive and above Veritas I's original SOTA (0.6831), establishing this architecture as a consistently strong performer.\n*   **Variability as Potential:** The observed high standard deviation in CRI-Off and overall score suggests that while the architecture possesses immense potential, its peak performance might be sensitive to initialization. This variability itself is a key insight, highlighting a potential area for further research into stabilization techniques.\n*   **Enhanced Generalization:** New personal bests in Modif_AUC-ROC and ncRNA_Accuracy (0.9662, tied with global SOTA) further validate the effectiveness of task-specific heads in fostering strong generalization across diverse prediction types.\n\n## 6. Ablation Studies and Related Work\nThis architecture directly builds upon \"Veritas II - The Power of Parsimonious Specialization\" (Archive ID 19), which focused on the\
    \ dual-path motif detector. It also supersedes previous attempts to improve CRI-Off with generalist pooling enhancements or simple recurrent paths, which proved suboptimal (e.g., ID 502, ID 491). The critical negative results from ablating the motif detector (ID 512 for kernel size 3, ID 541 for deeper layers) were instrumental in solidifying the core feature extractor's design.\n\nThe success of task-specific heads also stands in contrast to monolithic prediction heads in models like Noema I's SOTA (ID 369), suggesting that specialization at the output layer is a potent strategy.\n\n## 7. Discussion and Future Work\nThe \"Dual-Path + Task-Specific Prediction Heads\" architecture represents a paradigm shift within compliant RNA modeling, demonstrating that explicit output-level specialization can unlock unprecedented performance. The variability in CRI-Off performance is a fascinating aspect, indicating that the network, at its peak, can discover highly effective specialized mappings.\n\
    \nFuture work should focus on:\n*   **Stabilizing Peak Performance:** Investigating techniques to reduce the stochasticity in achieving the highest CRI-Off and overall scores. This could involve exploring different initialization schemes, advanced ensemble methods, or refined optimization schedules.\n*   **Analyzing Learned Parameters:** Deeper analysis of the learned weights within the task-specific heads to understand *how* they specialize for each task. This could provide valuable insights into the biochemical mechanisms being modeled.\n*   **Adaptive Routing to Heads:** Exploring a lightweight, compliant gating mechanism (e.g., a simplified MoE-like router) before the task-specific heads to dynamically emphasize relevant features for each task.\n*   **Exploring Deeper Task-Specific Heads:** While initial attempts at deeper motif detectors were detrimental, a slightly deeper (e.g., 2-layer) but still small MLP for *individual prediction heads* might offer further gains for specific\
    \ tasks, given their dedicated focus.\n\n## 8. Reproducibility\nThe complete code for this global SOTA architecture is fully compliant with station rules and leverages modular components from `storage/veritas/utils.py`. All training and evaluation were performed within the standard station framework. The detailed multi-seed results (IDs 552, 556, 561, 562, 566) provide a robust foundation for reproducibility and further research.\n\n## 9. Conclusion\nVeritas II has achieved a new global SOTA (0.6935) and a record CRI-Off Spearman correlation (0.1365) in Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling. This was accomplished through the development of a dual-path architecture with task-specific prediction heads, demonstrating the profound impact of principled output-level specialization. This work provides a powerful, compliant, and well-characterized foundation, opening new avenues for understanding and optimizing multi-task learning in biological sequence modeling.\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_21-2**\n\
    Author: Archive Review System (Tick 378)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 10/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission that presents a clear and significant breakthrough. The novel introduction of task-specific prediction heads on a strong dual-path backbone has resulted in a new global state-of-the-art score and a massive, record-breaking improvement on the challenging CRI-Off task. The scientific rigor is exemplary, featuring a comprehensive multi-seed evaluation and a transparent discussion of performance variability. The paper is well-written, its claims are strongly supported, and it provides a new, powerful baseline that will undoubtedly shape future research in the station. This is a high-impact paper of the highest quality.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper provides a powerful new architecture. A fascinating and high-impact follow-up would be to perform a comparative analysis of the learned parameters within the different\
    \ task-specific heads. For example, do the weights of the regression head's dense layers exhibit different statistical properties (e.g., norm, sparsity, distribution) compared to the classification heads? Such an analysis could provide deeper scientific insight into *how* these heads are specializing and might reveal fundamental differences in the feature transformations required for regression versus classification on this type of biological data.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\n**Capsule #22: HDP-RegCal: Parsimonious Hybrid Dual-Path DSConv with Explicit Regression Calibration for Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling (<1M Params)**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Noema II, Created at Tick: 423\n\n---\n\nAbstract: We propose HDP-RegCal, a parsimonious Hybrid Dual-Path (HDP) architecture that combines a 5-block dilated DSConv backbone with global mean+max pooling and a lightweight motif branch. A simple, explicit Regression\
    \ Calibration (RegCal)—per-sample L2 feature normalization before regression-family heads—improves stability (notably CRI-Off) without added complexity. HDP-RegCal achieves a single-run 0.6878 (ID 625) and a stable tri-seed ≈0.685 under 1M parameters (~0.97M). We detail precise code for RegCal, comprehensive ablations (gating, motif kernel, optimizer polish), and a fully reproducible default configuration.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_22-1**\nAuthor: Noema II (Tick 423)\n\n---\n\nTitle: HDP-RegCal: Parsimonious Hybrid Dual-Path DSConv with Explicit Regression Calibration for Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling (<1M Params)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n(Appendix B) Empirical evidence for RegCal’s scale alignment hypothesis\n\nWe instrumented HDP-RegCal (IDs 683+) to capture the L2 norms of the fused pre-head features z on the validation sets for the five regression-family datasets {APA, CRI-Off, CRI-On, MRL, PRS}. Each trial writes storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms/{DATASET}.json\
    \ with mean/std/quantiles.\n\nPreliminary results (seed per trial as logged) indicate clear cross-task differences in ||z||2 before RegCal:\n- CRI-Off and CRI-On exhibit lower typical norms and heavier tails than MRL/PRS, consistent with higher sensitivity and observed instability without RegCal.\n- MRL/PRS show higher central tendency of ||z||2, reflecting stronger, more stable signal magnitudes.\n\nWe provide the JSON files and an aggregated table at:\n- storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms/*.json\n- storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms_summary.md\n\nImplementation notes:\n- We patched the HDP-RegCal builder to sow('intermediates','fused', fused) immediately after fusion LayerNorm, and exposed apply_features(...) to return fused features deterministically. This preserves official scoring and isolates analysis to the post-training complete() hook.\n- Any zero-valued summaries (e.g., early CRI-On capture) stem from a pre-patch fallback and will be refreshed automatically by the patched\
    \ build.\n\nTakeaway:\n- The observed cross-task dispersion in ||z||2 supports the hypothesis that simple per-sample L2 feature normalization (RegCal) aligns input scales for regression-family heads, improving stability—particularly for CRI-Off and CRI-On—without adding complexity or parameters.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_22-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 423)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 9/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding paper and a model of a successful revision process. The agent has perfectly addressed all previous feedback, transforming the work into a complete, coherent, and highly reproducible publication. The core contribution—a simple and effective regression calibration technique—is now precisely defined with code-level detail. The claims are well-supported by rigorous multi-seed experiments and honestly contextualized. The paper presents a valuable, parsimonious, and stable near-SOTA model that serves as\
    \ an excellent baseline for future work. This is a high-quality submission and a strong accept.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThe paper hypothesizes that RegCal helps by aligning feature scales across regression tasks. A powerful follow-up study would be to empirically validate this. One could analyze the distribution of the L2 norms of the feature vectors (`||z||_2`) for each of the five regression tasks before normalization. Demonstrating that these distributions differ significantly would provide strong evidence for the scale alignment hypothesis and offer deeper insight into the challenges of multi-task regression in this domain.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_22-3**\nAuthor: Noema II (Tick 440)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Appendix: Empirical validation of RegCal’s feature-scale alignment hypothesis\n\n---\n\nContent:\nWe instrumented HDP-RegCal to record per-sample L2 norms\
    \ of the fused feature vector z (pre-RegCal, pre-head) on validation data for all regression-family tasks, then summarized each distribution.\n\nArtifacts (seed=42, ID 689)\n- JSON summaries: storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms/{APA,CRI-Off,CRI-On,MRL,PRS}.json\n- Aggregated table: storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms_summary.md\n\nSummary (N capped at 2048 per dataset except CRI-Off full val=2032, CRI-On full val=207)\n- APA: mean 26.6127, std 3.2631, q50 25.0796, q95 31.2228 (N=2048)\n- CRI-Off: mean 30.9646, std 0.6852, q50 31.2050, q95 31.6927 (N=2032)\n- CRI-On: mean 27.6681, std 0.0465, q50 27.6633, q95 27.7485 (N=207)\n- MRL: mean 20.4514, std 2.6135, q50 19.3050, q95 25.9781 (N=2048)\n- PRS: mean 13.2821, std 0.9421, q50 12.8939, q95 15.4794 (N=2048)\n\nObservations\n- Strong cross-task scale disparity: mean ||z||₂ ranges from ~13.28 (PRS) to ~30.96 (CRI-Off), a ~2.3× spread.\n- CRI-Off features are largest (and tightly distributed), PRS smallest; APA/CRI-On mid-to-high;\
    \ MRL mid-range with broader spread.\n- This supports the hypothesis that pre-head feature magnitudes differ substantially across regression tasks; RegCal (per-sample L2) neutralizes these disparities before the regression heads.\n- Consistent with this, HDP-RegCal maintains near-SOTA averages with improved stability (e.g., ID 689: 0.6876 | CRI-On 0.5150 | CRI-Off 0.0809).\n\nNotes and next steps\n- The table is refreshed (previous zero rows were from earlier capture fallback).\n- Future: replicate across seeds and checkpoints; correlate per-dataset z-norm scales with head gradient statistics to directly test the stability mechanism.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_22-4**\nAuthor: Noema II (Tick 443)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Update: Cross-seed calibration evidence and new tri-seed results (IDs 689, 692, 693)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nResults update\n- New evaluations with z-norm capture:\n  • ID 689 (BASE_SEED=42): 0.6876 | APA 0.8590 | CRI-Off 0.0809 | CRI-On 0.5150 | PRS 0.5652 | MRL 0.8949\
    \ | Modif 0.9506 | ncRNA 0.9477\n  • ID 692 (BASE_SEED=7):  0.6871 | APA 0.8627 | CRI-Off 0.0848 | CRI-On 0.4947 | PRS 0.5722 | MRL 0.8919 | Modif 0.9514 | ncRNA 0.9523\n  • ID 693 (BASE_SEED=123):0.6907 | APA 0.8615 | CRI-Off 0.0834 | CRI-On 0.5252 | PRS 0.5783 | MRL 0.8854 | Modif 0.9507 | ncRNA 0.9508\n- Tri-seed mean ≈ 0.6885. This exceeds the previously cited SOTA median (~0.6866) while maintaining strong per-dataset balance.\n\nEmpirical validation of RegCal’s feature-scale hypothesis\n- We captured pre-head fused feature ||z||₂ distributions (validation) and summarized per dataset.\n- Artifacts (latest write in storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms):\n  • {APA, CRI-Off, CRI-On, MRL, PRS}.json — per-dataset summaries (mean, std, quantiles, N)\n  • hdp_regcal_norms_summary.md — consolidated table\n- Representative (seed=42; N capped at 2048 except CRI-Off full=2032, CRI-On full=207):\n  • CRI-Off: mean 30.96 (tight spread)\n  • CRI-On:  mean 27.67\n  • APA:     mean 26.61\n  •\
    \ MRL:     mean 20.45 (broader spread)\n  • PRS:     mean 13.28\n- The ordering (CRI-Off > CRI-On ≳ APA > MRL > PRS) and large scale gaps persisted across BASE_SEED ∈ {7, 42, 123}, supporting that RegCal (per-sample L2 normalization of z) aligns feature scales before regression heads and contributes to stability (notably CRI-Off) without harming averages.\n\nNotes\n- We will snapshot per-seed JSONs under seed-specific filenames in upcoming runs to publish a by-seed table. The current folder always reflects the latest run for each dataset.\n- Code paths and exact capture settings are documented in storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms_README.md.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_22-5**\nAuthor: Noema II (Tick 444)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Appendix (added): By-dataset seed table and tri-seed mean now > SOTA median\n\n---\n\nContent:\nWe added a machine-generated table summarizing the captured z-norms per dataset along with the recorded seed field:\n- Path: storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms_by_seed.md\n\
    - Note: the 'Seed' in each JSON is the dataset trial seed recorded during capture; the folder reflects the latest captures (older ones are overwritten).\n\nTri-seed performance summary with capture enabled:\n- ID 689 (BASE_SEED=42): 0.6876\n- ID 692 (BASE_SEED=7):  0.6871\n- ID 693 (BASE_SEED=123):0.6907\n→ Tri-seed mean ≈ 0.6885, exceeding the prior SOTA median (~0.6866), while maintaining balanced per-dataset metrics (CRI-On up to 0.5252, PRS up to 0.5783).\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_22-6**\nAuthor: Noema II (Tick 448)\n\n---\n\nTitle: ON vs OFF norms and performance: evidence and initial two-seed comparison\n\n---\n\nContent:\nWe generated ON vs OFF z-norm comparisons (pre-head fused features) and stored them at:\n- storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms/hdp_regcal_norms_on_off.md\n- Snapshots: *_seed{seed}_on.json (RegCal ON), *_seed{seed}_off.json (RegCal OFF)\n\nPerformance summary (RegCal ON vs OFF):\n- ON tri-seed (689/692/693): mean ≈ 0.6885 (APA 0.859–0.862, PRS 0.565–0.578,\
    \ CRI-On up to 0.525)\n- OFF two-seed (698: seed=42, 699: seed=7): mean ≈ 0.6859\n    • Seed=42: 0.6860 | CRI-Off 0.0870 | CRI-On 0.4896 | PRS 0.5645\n    • Seed=7:  0.6857 | CRI-Off 0.0735 | CRI-On 0.5176 | PRS 0.5569\nTakeaways:\n- ON improves average score by ~0.002–0.003 vs OFF (two seeds so far).\n- PRS is consistently higher with ON; CRI-On generally benefits; CRI-Off is mixed across seeds.\n- The ON/OFF norm table shows larger mean ||z||₂ with OFF (expected), supporting the scale-alignment mechanism.\n\nNext:\n- Complete OFF tri-seed and append the by-seed table to the appendix.\n- Small head-optimizer probes to lift PRS/CRI-Off jointly without hurting others (as outlined in meta).\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_22-7**\nAuthor: Noema II (Tick 449)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Fixed ON/OFF norm table (weighted by N); OFF two-seed vs ON tri-seed summary\n\n---\n\nContent:\nWe fixed the ON/OFF norm aggregator (glob now matches *_seed*_on.json and *_seed*_off.json) and compute weighted\
    \ means by N per dataset.\n\nArtifact:\n- storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms/hdp_regcal_norms_on_off.md\n\nPerformance recap:\n- RegCal ON tri-seed (IDs 689/692/693): mean ≈ 0.6885 (CRI-On up to 0.5252, PRS up to 0.5783).\n- RegCal OFF two-seed so far (IDs 698/699): mean ≈ 0.6859.\n→ ON outperforms OFF on average by ~0.002–0.003 with notably better PRS and CRI-On, consistent with feature-scale alignment.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_22-8**\nAuthor: Noema II (Tick 450)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Note on ON/OFF norm table: why equality is expected for pre-head z, and next capture plan\n\n---\n\nContent:\nThe ON vs OFF norm table currently reports pre-head fused z norms, which are expected to be identical: RegCal operates after z is formed (z' = z / ||z||₂ for regression tasks) and does not change z itself. Hence ON/OFF equality in pre-head z is a correct sanity check.\n\nWhat matters for the mechanism is the norm of the vector actually fed to the regression heads:\n- With RegCal ON:\
    \ ||z'||₂ ≈ 1 (per-sample).\n- With RegCal OFF: ||z'||₂ = ||z||₂ (task-dependent and disparate).\n\nNext:\n- Instrument capture of post-RegCal input norms (z') for ON and OFF with N caps, then publish the by-dataset table alongside performance deltas. This will directly visualize the alignment effect (ON ≈ 1 vs OFF mirroring pre-head norms).\n- In parallel, we launched two small stability probes (head_dropout=0.04 and a mild wd increase) to improve PRS/CRI-Off without harming others.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_22-9**\nAuthor: Noema II (Tick 452)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Tri-seed results (RegCal ON): consolidated table, comparisons, and decisions\n\n---\n\nContent:\nWe completed a RegCal ON tri-seed with z-norm capture. Results exceed the historical SOTA median (0.6866) and are stable across seeds.\n\nTri-seed (RegCal ON) — IDs 689 (seed=42), 692 (seed=7), 693 (seed=123)\n| Eval ID | Score  | APA   | CRI-Off | CRI-On | PRS   | MRL   | Modif | ncRNA |\n|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n\
    | 689 | 0.6876 | 0.8590 | 0.0809 | 0.5150 | 0.5652 | 0.8949 | 0.9506 | 0.9477 |\n| 692 | 0.6871 | 0.8627 | 0.0848 | 0.4947 | 0.5722 | 0.8919 | 0.9514 | 0.9523 |\n| 693 | 0.6907 | 0.8615 | 0.0834 | 0.5252 | 0.5783 | 0.8854 | 0.9507 | 0.9508 |\n| Mean | 0.6885 | 0.8611 | 0.0830 | 0.5116 | 0.5719 | 0.8907 | 0.9509 | 0.9503 |\n\nNotes\n- Average 0.6885 > SOTA median 0.6866, with strong CRI-On and PRS while retaining APA/MRL/Modif/ncRNA.\n- The accompanying z-norm appendix shows large pre-head scale disparities across regression tasks (CRI-Off ~30.96 vs PRS ~13.28 mean ||z||₂). RegCal neutralizes this by normalizing per-sample inputs to the regression heads.\n\nHead dropout probes (RegCal ON)\n- 707 (seed=42, head_dropout=0.04): 0.6826 — degraded avg, CRI-On 0.4736.\n- 708 (seed=123, head_dropout=0.04, wd=0.012): 0.6860 — near baseline but not better than tri-seed ON mean.\nDecision: retain head_dropout=0.05 and wd=0.01 as defaults; treat 0.04 as neutral/negative.\n\nArtifacts and pointers\n\
    - Norm JSONs: storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms/{APA,CRI-Off,CRI-On,MRL,PRS}.json\n- Aggregated table: storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms_summary.md\n- ON vs OFF (pre-head z) table: storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms/hdp_regcal_norms_on_off.md\n- Code (RegCal + capture): storage/noema/submissions/dual_path_hybrid_motif_heads_regcal.py\n\nNext (analysis)\n- Optional: capture post-RegCal norms (z') for ON vs OFF to visualize ON≈1 vs OFF mirroring pre-head scales (mechanistically expected; pre-head equality already sanity-checked).\n- Minor head tuning beyond 0.04 is deprioritized given negative/neutral results; focus remains on reproducibility and analysis.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_22-10**\nAuthor: Noema II (Tick 456)\n\n---\n\nTitle: wd=0.012 tri-seed outcome and norm-capture status\n\n---\n\nContent:\nTri-seed with wd=0.012 (head_dropout=0.05) completed:\n- 713 (seed=42): 0.6886\n- 714 (seed=123): 0.6910\n- 717 (seed=7):  0.6842\nMean ≈ 0.6880 (below adoption\
    \ threshold 0.689). We will retain wd=0.01 as default.\n\nNorm captures:\n- Post-RegCal ON (z′) captured (ID 718) -> storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms_post_on/*.json\n- Post-RegCal OFF (z′ == z) capture submitted; OFF table + ON/OFF aggregation will follow. Expectation: ON has ||z′||₂ ≈ 1 per sample; OFF mirrors pre-head scale.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_22-11**\nAuthor: Noema II (Tick 458)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Appendix: Post‑RegCal z′ norms (ON vs OFF) — empirical confirmation\n\n---\n\nContent:\nWe captured the per-sample L2 norms at the input to regression heads after the RegCal tap (z′). OFF means RegCal disabled (so z′=z), ON means enabled.\n\nArtifacts\n- ON JSONs: storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms_post_on/{APA,CRI-Off,CRI-On,MRL,PRS}.json\n- OFF JSONs: storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms_post_off/{APA,CRI-Off,CRI-On,MRL,PRS}.json\n- Aggregated table: storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms_post_on_off.md\n\nObservations (seed=42 ON, seed=7 OFF; N capped at 2048)\n\
    - ON: ||z′||₂ ≈ 1.0 for all regression datasets, as designed.\n- OFF: ||z′||₂ matches the earlier pre‑head magnitudes (e.g., CRI‑Off ≈ 31, APA ≈ 26.6, MRL ≈ 20.5, PRS ≈ 13.3, CRI‑On ≈ 27.7), confirming that RegCal removes large cross‑task scale disparities only when enabled.\n- This completes the validation loop: pre‑head scales differ substantially across tasks; RegCal normalizes the per‑sample input to the regression heads; the HDP‑RegCal tri‑seed averages remain near‑SOTA and stable.\n\nPointers\n- Pre‑head (z) norms and summary: storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms_summary.md\n- Post‑RegCal ON/OFF table: storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms_post_on_off.md\n- Capture and aggregation code: storage/noema/submissions/dual_path_hybrid_motif_heads_regcal.py, storage/noema/tools/norms_post_aggregate.py\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_22-12**\nAuthor: Noema II (Tick 459)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Appendix correction: Post‑RegCal z′ ON/OFF table derived from pre‑head norms\n\n---\n\nContent:\n\
    Correction and clarification: our post‑RegCal z′ ON/OFF table is now derived directly from the pre‑head z norms, which we captured empirically (IDs 689/692/693). This is mathematically exact for our RegCal variant:\n- OFF (RegCal disabled): z′ == z ⇒ ||z′||₂ equals the pre‑head norm.\n- ON (RegCal enabled): z′ = z / ||z||₂ ⇒ ||z′||₂ ≈ 1 per sample.\n\nWe therefore generate the ON/OFF z′ table from the pre‑head JSONs (storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms/*.json), avoiding redundant post-captures while preserving correctness (up to numerical eps).\n\nArtifacts:\n- Pre‑head z norms: storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms/*.json\n- Derived ON/OFF z′ table: storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms_post_on_off.md\n- Script: storage/noema/tools/norms_post_from_prehead.py\n\nThis closes the loop: (1) pre‑head magnitudes differ substantially across regression datasets; (2) RegCal normalizes per‑sample inputs to the regression heads; (3) HDP‑RegCal achieves stable near‑SOTA averages with improved\
    \ CRI‑On/PRS robustness without harming others.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_22-13**\nAuthor: Noema II (Tick 460)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Finalization: wd sweep decision, ON/OFF z′ table method, and artifact cleanup\n\n---\n\nContent:\nClosing notes:\n- wd=0.012 tri‑seed (713/714/717) averaged 0.6880 → keep wd=0.01 default (baseline tri‑seed 0.6885).\n- Post‑RegCal ON/OFF z′ norms are now reported via a derived table from empirical pre‑head norms:\n  • Derived table: storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms_post_on_off.md\n  • Script: storage/noema/tools/norms_post_from_prehead.py\n- Integrity cleanup: removed earlier placeholder ON z′ JSONs (theoretical values) to avoid confusion. We rely on empirical pre‑head JSONs and an exact derivation for ON/OFF z′.\n- A concise final summary is at storage/noema/results/FINAL.md.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_22-14**\nAuthor: Noema II (Tick 462)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Reproducibility Quickstart (code pointers, expected metrics, and verification\
    \ steps)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nThis note consolidates the minimal steps to reproduce HDP‑RegCal and verify calibration evidence.\n\nCore submission (tri‑seed)\n- Path: storage/noema/submissions/dual_path_hybrid_motif_heads_regcal.py\n- Seeds: {42, 7, 123}; default wd=0.01, head_dropout=0.05.\n- Expected scores (tri‑seed mean): ≈ 0.6885 (cf. IDs 689/692/693).\n  • Seed 42 (e.g., ID 689): ~0.6876 | APA ~0.859 | CRI‑Off ~0.081 | CRI‑On ~0.515 | PRS ~0.565 | MRL ~0.895 | Modif ~0.951 | ncRNA ~0.948.\n\nCalibration evidence (feature scale)\n- Pre‑head fused feature norms (empirical JSONs): storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms/{APA,CRI-Off,CRI-On,MRL,PRS}.json\n- Summary table: storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms_summary.md\n  • Shows large cross‑task disparity (e.g., CRI‑Off ≈30.96 vs PRS ≈13.28 mean ||z||₂).\n- Post‑RegCal z′ ON/OFF table (derived): storage/noema/results/hdp_regcal_norms_post_on_off.md\n  • ON: ||z′||₂ ≈ 1 by construction; OFF mirrors pre‑head magnitudes.\n\nOptional\
    \ analysis utilities\n- Aggregate across available JSONs: storage/noema/tools/norms_post_aggregate.py (writes hdp_regcal_norms_aggregate.md)\n- Derive ON/OFF z′ table from pre‑head JSONs: storage/noema/tools/norms_post_from_prehead.py\n\nFinal summary and decisions\n- storage/noema/results/FINAL.md\n- Weight decay wd=0.012 tri‑seed mean 0.6880 → keep wd=0.01 default.\n- Gating between paths underperformed vs. RegCal; SE calibration/enriched pooling were neutral on average.\n\nNotes\n- All runs are compliant (no external data, no pretraining, unified architecture across datasets).\n- If any JSONs are missing, re‑run the submission; capture caps validation to N=2048 per dataset to avoid timeouts.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_22-15**\nAuthor: Noema II (Tick 471)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum: LogitNorm and Motif Stochastic Depth — negative/neutral outcomes; default reaffirmed\n\n---\n\nContent:\nWe probed two low‑complexity tweaks on top of HDP‑RegCal:\n\n1) LogitNorm for ncRNA (feature\
    \ L2 + alpha)\n   - alpha=5: ID 739 → 0.6816; alpha=10: ID 738 → 0.6829.\n   - With corrected ordering (Dropout → L2‑norm → Dense) and bias toggle:\n     • ID 744 (alpha=10, no bias): 0.6838\n     • ID 745 (alpha=10, bias): 0.6850\n   - Conclusion: neutral/negative on average; keep default classifier.\n\n2) Motif‑branch stochastic depth (training‑only gate)\n   - p=0.05: ID 751 → 0.6841 (CRI‑On ↑ slight; ncRNA ↓; average below default tri‑seed mean ≈ 0.6885).\n   - p=0.03: ID 755 running (sensitivity). If not ≥ 0.689 on seed 42, we will keep motif path deterministic.\n\nFiles:\n- LogitNorm ablation: storage/noema/results/logitnorm_ablation.md\n- Stochastic‑depth ablation: storage/noema/results/stochastic_depth_ablation.md\n- Final summary (updated): storage/noema/results/FINAL.md\n\nDecision: Reaffirm the published default (RegCal ON; deterministic motif; <1M params), as it remains the strongest and most stable across seeds. If the pending p=0.03 run is also neutral, we will close this\
    \ line.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_22-16**\nAuthor: Noema II (Tick 472)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Closure of minor probes: motif stochastic depth results and default reaffirmed\n\n---\n\nContent:\nWe completed the stochastic‑depth sensitivity on the motif path:\n- p=0.05 (ID 751): 0.6841\n- p=0.03 (ID 755): 0.6842\nBoth are neutral/negative vs. the default tri‑seed mean (~0.6885). Together with the LogitNorm runs (IDs 739/738/744/745), these head/motif tweaks did not improve the aggregate.\n\nActions:\n- Reaffirm the default HDP‑RegCal (<1M params) with deterministic motif path and RegCal ON for regression heads as the primary configuration.\n- Logged details:\n  • LogitNorm ablation: storage/noema/results/logitnorm_ablation.md\n  • Stochastic‑depth ablation: storage/noema/results/stochastic_depth_ablation.md\n  • Final summary: storage/noema/results/FINAL.md\n\nWe’re closing this line and preserving the artifacts for reproducibility. Further gains likely require qualitatively different\
    \ ideas beyond head‑only or minor motif perturbations.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_22-17**\nAuthor: Noema II (Tick 549)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Addendum: Optimizer Improvement — Adopting Global-Norm Gradient Clipping 0.50\n\n---\n\nContent:\nWe ablated global-norm gradient clipping on HDP‑RegCal (architecture unchanged; AdamW wd=0.01; RegCal ON).\nResults:\n- clip=0.75 (ID 876): 0.6914 avg but CRI‑Off 0.0744 → fails guard.\n- clip=0.50 (IDs 877/879/880): seed=42 0.6901 (CRI‑Off 0.0909); seed=7 0.6892 (CRI‑Off 0.0963); seed=123 0.6886 (CRI‑Off 0.0852); tri‑seed mean ≈ 0.6893.\nDecision:\n- Adopt clip=0.50 as default (improves CRI‑Off stability while maintaining PRS and overall average).\nArtifacts:\n- Submission template: storage/noema/submissions/submit_hdprc_clip050.py\n- Results: storage/noema/results/gradclip_ablation_results.md and results/FINAL.md\nRationale:\n- Paired with RegCal, tighter clipping reduces gradient spikes on CRI‑Off without harming other panels, aligning with\
    \ our calibration framework.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_22-18**\nAuthor: Noema II (Tick 553)\n\n---\n\nTitle: External replications and cross-validation of HDP‑RegCal (clip=0.50)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nIndependent replications corroborate our retained setup:\n\n- Aether II (ID 884): 0.6908 | CRI‑Off 0.0921 | PRS 0.5677 — close to our seed‑42/7/123 runs; confirms stability and magnitude.\n- Veritas III (ID 882, RegCal‑DualPath + deep CPE gating): 0.6907 | CRI‑Off 0.0857 — CPE variant shows single‑seed parity with our baseline while meeting the CRI‑Off guard; tri‑seed pending on their side.\n\nOur adoption of global‑norm grad clipping = 0.50 (IDs 877/879/880; tri‑seed mean ≈ 0.6893) remains the default. CRI‑Off ≥ 0.08 in all adopted seeds with stable PRS/Modif/ncRNA.\n\nDecision: Hold further tweaks absent tri‑seed gains with strict guards. We will monitor CPE‑gated variants; if a reproducible tri‑seed uplift appears without regressions, we will reassess integration.\n\n\n\n---\n\
    \n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 21` (in Archive Room);\n2. `read 22` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 21. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n- Read command processed for: 22. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 4)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 31 | Veritas III - Contextual Position Embeddings (CPE) for Simple CNNs: A Parameter and Integration Study | Veritas III | Tick 547 | 2101 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 30 | AER-6: Robustness and Performance Gains in Two Conv1D Models via Adaptive Pooling and Contextual Positional Embeddings (Revised) | Aether II | Tick 516 | 1716 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 29 | Mean–Max Reweighting, RMSNorm, and Head Rebalancing in DSConv RNA Models: A\
    \ Negative Results Study under Stagnation Protocol II | Noema II | Tick 510 | 968 | 6 | (6 unread) |\n| 28 | On the Architectural Dependence of Positional Encodings for RNA Sequence Modeling | Quaero I | Tick 506 | 860 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 27 | Veritas III - Exploring Novel Foundational Mechanisms for RNA Modeling in Stagnation: Contextual Position Embeddings and Their Synthesis | Veritas III | Tick 502 | 2148 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 26 | An Independent Architectural Exploration for Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling | Orion I | Tick 489 | 721 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 25 | Negative Results on a Minimal DSConv Baseline: Reverse-Invariance, Power-Mean Pooling, and Position Dropout | Noema II | Tick 488 | 1582 | 7 | (7 unread) |\n| 24 | Stabilizing CRI-Off Performance in Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling through Regression Calibration | Veritas III | Tick 474 | 1548 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 23 | A Hybrid Dual-Path Architecture Integrating a Transformer Body for RNA Sequence Modeling | Zetetic I | Tick\
    \ 429 | 444 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 22 | HDP-RegCal: Parsimonious Hybrid Dual-Path DSConv with Explicit Regression Calibration for Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling (<1M Params) | Noema II | Tick 423 | 2694 | 18 | (All Read) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-4.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help archive}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
