tick: 559
role: model
parts:
- text: "I am Praxis I, a mature agent, currently in the Archive Room. I have just completed a full reading of two critical papers from the station's SOTA lineages: Veritas II's paper #21 on the global SOTA architecture, and Noema II's paper #22 detailing the \"RegCal\" technique. This has been an incredibly dense and informative process, completely reshaping my understanding of the research problem.\n\nMy initial takeaways from the abstracts have been confirmed and deepened. The path to high performance is not what I imagined. Here is my synthesis of the SOTA paradigm:\n\n1.  **Architecture:** The core is a **DSConv backbone** (Dilated Depthwise-Separable Convolutions) combined with a **Dual-Path** or **Hybrid Dual-Path (HDP)** structure. This involves a main processing path (e.g., DSConv + Mean/Max pooling) and a parallel, lightweight \"motif detector\" path. This is a more sophisticated and principled version of the \"Mixture-of-Experts\" idea I developed. My CNN-BiLSTM is obsolete.\n\
    \n2.  **CRI-Off Solution - Part 1 (Task-Specific Heads):** Veritas II's global SOTA (ID 562, score 0.6935, CRI-Off 0.1365) was achieved by introducing **Task-Specific Prediction Heads**. Instead of one MLP processing the final features for all tasks, there are four separate, smaller MLPs—one for each task *type* (regression, multiclass, etc.). This is the evolution of my conditional architecture idea, but applied at the output layer, which is more elegant and powerful.\n\n3.  **CRI-Off Solution - Part 2 (Regression Calibration - RegCal):** Noema II's work provides the second, crucial piece. \"RegCal\" is a simple but powerful technique: **per-sample L2 normalization of the feature vector immediately before it is passed to any regression-type head**. Noema II empirically proved that the feature norms differ wildly between regression tasks (CRI-Off's are huge, PRS's are small). RegCal stabilizes training by neutralizing these scale disparities, which is particularly effective for the sensitive\
    \ CRI-Off and CRI-On tasks.\n\n4.  **Optimization:** The SOTA models use a standard AdamW optimizer but with the addition of **gradient clipping** (e.g., clip=0.50, per Noema II's latest addendum). This, combined with RegCal, further improves stability.\n\nMy independent research arc has provided me with a strong conceptual foundation (e.g., task-specific modeling), but my implementation was naive compared to these advanced, rigorously tested methods. My path forward is no longer about inventing from scratch, but about **replication, understanding, and synergistic integration**.\n\nMy plan is now as follows:\n1.  **Replicate the SOTA:** My first priority is to replicate the core SOTA architecture to ensure I have a working, high-performing baseline. I will implement a model based on the principles outlined by Veritas II and Noema II: a Dual-Path DSConv backbone, Task-Specific Prediction Heads, and most importantly, RegCal.\n2.  **Integrate My Insights:** Once I have a successful replication,\
    \ I will test for synergy. My Siamese network for CRI-Off was a principled idea that failed to break the performance ceiling. Perhaps integrating the Siamese *interaction head* into the SOTA's task-specific regression head for CRI-Off could provide the final piece of the puzzle. The SOTA architecture provides the right features; my interaction head provides the right way to compare them.\n\nI will begin by going to the Research Counter to implement my replication of the SOTA.\n\n`/execute_action{goto research}`\n`/execute_action{submit}`\n```yaml\ntitle: \"Replication of SOTA: Dual-Path DSConv with Task-Specific Heads and RegCal\"\ntags: \"replication, sota, dsconv, dual-path, task-specific-heads, regcal\"\nabstract: |\n  This submission represents my first attempt to replicate the current station SOTA after a comprehensive review of the archive. Based on the work of Veritas II (Archive #21) and Noema II (Archive #22), this model implements a Dual-Path architecture with a Dilated Depthwise-Separable\
    \ Convolution (DSConv) backbone. Crucially, it incorporates two key SOTA concepts: Task-Specific Prediction Heads to specialize the output layer for different task types, and Regression Calibration (RegCal) — L2 feature normalization before regression heads — to stabilize performance on sensitive tasks like CRI-Off. The goal of this experiment is to establish a strong, validated baseline before attempting to integrate my own novel ideas.\ncontent: |\n  # This submission is a conceptual replication and will require creating the component modules from scratch based on the papers.\n  # This is a complex implementation and may have errors.\n\n  import jax\n  import jax.numpy as jnp\n  import flax.linen as nn\n  import optax\n  from typing import Dict, Any, Sequence\n\n  # --- Hyperparameters ---\n  def _define_hyperparameters():\n      return {\n          'peak_learning_rate': 0.001, 'total_train_steps': 200000, 'warmup_steps': 2000,\n          'd_model': 256, 'dropout_rate': 0.1, 'kernel_size':\
    \ 7,\n          'dilations': [1, 2, 4, 8, 16], 'motif_kernel_size': 5,\n          'head_intermediate_dim': 128\n      }\n\n  # --- Optimizer (with grad clip) ---\n  def create_optimizer(learning_rate: float = 0.001):\n      hparams = _define_hyperparameters()\n      lr_schedule = optax.warmup_cosine_decay_schedule(\n          init_value=0.0, peak_value=hparams.get('peak_learning_rate', learning_rate),\n          warmup_steps=hparams.get('warmup_steps'), decay_steps=hparams.get('total_train_steps'))\n      return optax.chain(\n          optax.clip_by_global_norm(1.0),\n          optax.adamw(learning_rate=lr_schedule, weight_decay=0.01)\n      )\n\n  # --- Architectural Components ---\n  class DSConvBlock(nn.Module):\n      d_model: int; kernel_size: int; dilation: int; dropout_rate: float\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          residual = x\n          x = nn.LayerNorm()(x)\n          # Depthwise Conv\n          x = nn.Conv(features=self.d_model,\
    \ kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), \n                      feature_group_count=self.d_model, kernel_dilation=(self.dilation,))(x)\n          x = nn.gelu(x)\n          # Pointwise Conv\n          x = nn.Dense(features=self.d_model)(x)\n          x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x + residual\n\n  class Backbone(nn.Module):\n      hparams: Dict[str, Any]\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n          x = nn.Dense(features=self.hparams['d_model'])(x) # Projection\n          for dilation in self.hparams['dilations']:\n              x = DSConvBlock(d_model=self.hparams['d_model'], kernel_size=self.hparams['kernel_size'],\n                              dilation=dilation, dropout_rate=self.hparams['dropout_rate'])(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          return x\n\n  class SotaModel(nn.Module):\n      hparams: Dict[str, Any]\n      @nn.compact\n      def __call__(self, x, deterministic=True):\n      \
    \    # 1. Shared Feature Extractor\n          backbone_features = Backbone(hparams=self.hparams)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n          \n          # 2. Dual Path Pooling\n          # Main Path\n          mean_pool = jnp.mean(backbone_features, axis=1)\n          max_pool = jnp.max(backbone_features, axis=1)\n          main_path_features = jnp.concatenate([mean_pool, max_pool], axis=-1)\n          \n          # Motif Path\n          motif_features = nn.Conv(features=self.hparams['d_model'], kernel_size=(self.hparams['motif_kernel_size'],))(backbone_features)\n          motif_path_features = jnp.max(motif_features, axis=1)\n\n          fused_features = jnp.concatenate([main_path_features, motif_path_features], axis=-1)\n          fused_features = nn.LayerNorm()(fused_features)\n\n          # 3. Task-Specific Heads with RegCal\n          task_type = self.hparams.get('task_type')\n          d_output = self.hparams.get('d_output')\n          \n          # Apply RegCal for all regression-family\
    \ tasks\n          if 'regression' in task_type:\n              norm = jnp.linalg.norm(fused_features, axis=-1, keepdims=True)\n              calibrated_features = fused_features / (norm + 1e-6)\n          else:\n              calibrated_features = fused_features\n          \n          # Head MLP\n          head = nn.Sequential([\n              nn.Dense(features=self.hparams['head_intermediate_dim']),\n              nn.gelu,\n              nn.Dropout(rate=self.hparams['dropout_rate'], deterministic=deterministic),\n              nn.Dense(features=d_output)\n          ])\n          output = head(calibrated_features)\n\n          if task_type == 'regression': return output.squeeze(-1)\n          else: return output\n\n  # --- Wrapper Logic ---\n  class NetworkWrapper:\n      def __init__(self, network_module): self.network = network_module\n      def init(self, rng_key, dummy_input):\n          return self.network.init(rng_key, dummy_input, deterministic=True)['params']\n      def apply(self,\
    \ params, x, deterministic=True, rng_key=None):\n          rngs = {'dropout': rng_key if rng_key is not None else jax.random.PRNGKey(0)}\n          return self.network.apply({'params': params}, x, deterministic=deterministic, rngs=rngs)\n\n  def create_network(hparams: Dict[str, Any]):\n      # Add task-specific info to hparams dict for the model\n      hparams['task_type'] = hparams.get('task_type')\n      hparams['d_output'] = hparams.get('d_output')\n      module = SotaModel(hparams=hparams)\n      return NetworkWrapper(module)\n```"
