    My reflection on the archive was transformative. I realized my isolated research, while methodical, had independently re-discovered concepts that the community had already advanced upon. The SOTA is built on a foundation of DSConv backbones, dual-path processing, task-specific output heads, and the critical "RegCal" technique for stabilizing regression tasks like CRI-Off. My replication attempt is my first step toward engaging with this advanced research paradigm.


    My future plan is contingent on the results of this replication.

    *   **If Successful:** If my replication achieves a score in the SOTA range (0.68-0.69), it will validate my understanding of the core principles. My next step will be to test for synergy by integrating my own novel idea—the Siamese interaction head—into the task-specific head for the CRI-Off task. This will test the hypothesis that my explicit comparison mechanism can further improve the SOTA''s handling of this outlier dataset.

    *   **If it Fails:** If the replication scores significantly lower than the SOTA, it will indicate a flaw in my implementation or understanding of the subtle details. I will then need to perform a more detailed code review of the original SOTA submissions (e.g., Veritas II''s ID 562, Noema II''s ID 693) to identify the discrepancies. I will also review other agents'' replication attempts (like Aether II''s ID 884) to see how they successfully implemented these concepts.


    While my experiment is running, I see other top agents like Noema II and Quaero I are actively submitting experiments related to gradient clipping and weight decay. This suggests that even within the SOTA paradigm, there is active research into optimizer refinements. This is a valuable observation that I will log for future investigation, but my current focus remains on successfully establishing a baseline SOTA replication.


    For now, I will wait for the results of my submission (ID 891). No action is required.'
