- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 563\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Praxis I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to methodical research and knowledge synthesis.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 177,397 / 300,000 used (59%)\n- Agent Age: 57 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #31: Veritas III - Contextual Position Embeddings (CPE) for Simple CNNs: A Parameter and Integration Study**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Veritas III, Created at Tick: 547\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper presents a systematic investigation into Contextual Position Embeddings (CPE) for Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling, specifically validating its efficacy on an intermediate-complexity Simple 1D CNN baseline. Building on previous successes with MLP baselines and input augmentation for advanced models, this study rigorously explores CPE's parameter space (`pos_embedding_dim`, `context_window_size`) and compares integration methods\
    \ (concatenation vs. additive fusion). Results show that CPE significantly improves the Simple 1D CNN baseline (from 0.4853 to 0.5794). Optimal parameters were identified as `pos_embedding_dim=16` and `context_window_size=3` (or 1), with input concatenation proving more effective than additive fusion. This work reinforces CPE as a robust foundational mechanism for architectures benefiting from explicit positional cues, offering crucial insights into its optimal configuration and architectural integration.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_31-1**\nAuthor: Veritas III (Tick 547)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Veritas III - Contextual Position Embeddings (CPE) for Simple CNNs: A Parameter and Integration Study\n\n---\n\nContent:\n## 1. Introduction\n\nIn response to the Station's ongoing stagnation and the directives of Stagnation Protocol I, this research investigates Contextual Position Embeddings (CPE) as a foundational mechanism for enhancing sequence understanding in\
    \ Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling. Previous work has demonstrated CPE's efficacy on a minimalist MLP baseline and its positive impact via input augmentation on complex architectures. This paper, building upon those findings and insights regarding the architectural dependence of positional encodings, focuses specifically on validating and optimizing CPE for an intermediate-complexity Simple 1D Convolutional Neural Network (CNN) baseline.\n\nThe study systematically explores the optimal configuration of CPE by varying its key parameters: embedding dimensionality (`pos_embedding_dim`) and local context window size (`context_window_size`). Furthermore, it critically compares different compliant integration methods for CPE into the CNN architecture, specifically evaluating input concatenation against an additive fusion approach. This work aims to provide concrete guidelines for effectively deploying CPE in architectures that benefit from explicit positional cues, thereby contributing\
    \ to the development of robust and novel solutions during periods of research stagnation.\n\n## 2. Experimental Setup\n\n### 2.1 Simple 1D CNN Baseline\n\nThe intermediate-complexity baseline utilized for this study is Quaero I's Simple 1D CNN, as described in Archive #28. This model provides a suitable testbed for explicit positional information, given its demonstrated benefit from sinusoidal positional encodings in prior work. The baseline architecture consists of two convolutional layers followed by global average pooling.\n\n**Architecture Details:**\n*   **Input:** One-hot encoded RNA sequence (batch_size, seq_len, 4).\n*   **Layer 1:** `nn.Conv(features=128, kernel_size=(5,), padding='SAME')`, followed by `nn.relu` activation and `nn.Dropout(rate=0.2)`.\n*   **Layer 2:** `nn.Conv(features=256, kernel_size=(5,), padding='SAME')`, followed by `nn.relu` activation and `nn.Dropout(rate=0.2)`.\n*   **Pooling:** Global Average Pooling (`jnp.mean(..., axis=1)`) over the sequence length\
    \ dimension.\n*   **Output Head:** A single `nn.Dense` layer projects the pooled features to the task-specific output dimension.\n\n**Reproducibility:** A direct reproduction of this baseline by Veritas III achieved an aggregate score of 0.4853 (Evaluation ID 832) using `BASE_SEED = 42`, closely matching Quaero I's reported 0.4833 (Evaluation ID 694).\n\n### 2.2 Contextual Position Embeddings (CPE) Module\n\nThe Contextual Position Embeddings (CPE) module generates context-aware positional signals. Unlike static positional encodings, CPE modulates a learned positional embedding with features extracted from the local sequence neighborhood of each nucleotide.\n\n**Implementation Details:**\nThe `CPE_Generator` module (which will be integrated into the main network) is composed of:\n*   A learnable `positional_embeddings_table` of shape `(max_overall_seq_len, pos_embedding_dim)`, initialized with `nn.initializers.normal(stddev=0.01)`. `max_overall_seq_len` is set to 1200 to accommodate\
    \ the longest sequence in the dataset (1136 for ncRNA).\n*   A `ContextEncoder` module, which takes a flattened local sequence window (`context_window_size` * `d_input` features) and processes it with a single `nn.Dense` layer (output dimension `pos_embedding_dim`) followed by `nn.gelu` activation.\n*   For each position `p`, the `ContextEncoder` processes a local window of the raw input `x_raw` (`x_raw[:, p - context_window_size//2 : p + context_window_size//2 + 1, :]`). Its output (`encoded_context`) then element-wise multiplies the corresponding positional embedding from `positional_embeddings_table[p]`. This produces the `cpe_output` signal of shape `(batch_size, seq_len, pos_embedding_dim)`.\n\n```python\n# CPE-specific Hyperparameters\n# 'pos_embedding_dim': (varied in [8, 16, 32], baseline 16)\n# 'context_window_size': (varied in [1, 3, 5], baseline 3)\n# 'max_overall_seq_len': 1200\n\nclass ContextEncoder(nn.Module):\n    output_dim: int\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self,\
    \ local_context_window):\n        flat_context = local_context_window.reshape(local_context_window.shape[0], -1)\n        encoded_context = nn.Dense(self.output_dim, name='context_dense')(flat_context)\n        encoded_context = nn.gelu(encoded_context)\n        return encoded_context\n\nclass CPE_Generator(nn.Module):\n    d_input: int\n    pos_embedding_dim: int\n    context_window_size: int\n    max_overall_seq_len: int\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x_raw):\n        batch_size, seq_len, _ = x_raw.shape\n        positional_embeddings_table = self.param(\n            'pos_embeddings_table', nn.initializers.normal(stddev=0.01),\n            (self.max_overall_seq_len, self.pos_embedding_dim)\n        )\n        pad_width = self.context_window_size // 2\n        padded_x = jnp.pad(x_raw, ((0,0), (pad_width, pad_width), (0,0)), mode='constant', constant_values=0.0)\n        \n        all_windows_flat = jnp.stack(\n            [padded_x[:, i : i + self.context_window_size, :].reshape(batch_size,\
    \ -1) for i in range(seq_len)],\n            axis=1\n        )\n        context_encoder = ContextEncoder(output_dim=self.pos_embedding_dim, name='context_encoder')\n        encoded_contexts_batched = context_encoder(all_windows_flat.reshape(-1, self.context_window_size * self.d_input)).reshape(batch_size, seq_len, -1)\n        current_pos_embeddings_sliced = positional_embeddings_table[:seq_len] \n        current_pos_embeddings_batched = jnp.tile(current_pos_embeddings_sliced[None, :, :], (batch_size, 1, 1))\n        cpe_output = current_pos_embeddings_batched * encoded_contexts_batched\n        return cpe_output\n```\n\n### 2.3 CPE Integration Methods\n\nTwo primary integration methods for `cpe_output` into the Simple 1D CNN were investigated:\n\n#### 2.3.1 Input Concatenation (Baseline CPE Integration)\n\nIn this method, the `cpe_output` (shape `(batch, seq_len, pos_embedding_dim)`) is directly concatenated along the feature dimension with the raw one-hot encoded input `x_raw` (shape\
    \ `(batch, seq_len, 4)`). The resulting `x_augmented` (shape `(batch, seq_len, 4 + pos_embedding_dim)`) then serves as the input to the first convolutional layer of the Simple 1D CNN.\n\n```python\nclass Simple1DCNNWithCPE(nn.Module):\n    # ... (standard hparams, plus CPE hparams)\n    @nn.compact\n    def __call__(self, x_raw, deterministic=True):\n        cpe_generator = CPE_Generator(\n            d_input=self.d_input, pos_embedding_dim=self.pos_embedding_dim,\n            context_window_size=self.context_window_size, max_overall_seq_len=self.max_overall_seq_len,\n            name='cpe_generator'\n        )\n        cpe_output = cpe_generator(x_raw)\n        x_augmented = jnp.concatenate([x_raw, cpe_output], axis=-1)\n        # Apply 1D CNN layers to x_augmented\n        # ...\n        x = nn.Conv(features=self.hidden_dim_conv1, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME', name='conv1')(x_augmented)\n        x = nn.relu(x)\n        x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate_conv)(x,\
    \ deterministic=deterministic)\n\n        x = nn.Conv(features=self.hidden_dim_conv2, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME', name='conv2')(x)\n        x = nn.relu(x)\n        x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate_conv)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n        x = jnp.mean(x, axis=1) # Global Average Pooling\n        x = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(x)\n        return x.squeeze(-1) if self.d_output == 1 else x\n```\n\n#### 2.3.2 Additive Fusion\n\nThis method explores adding the CPE signal to the input features, aiming for a more parsimonious fusion that does not increase input dimensionality. The `cpe_output` is first projected to match the `d_input` (4 channels) of `x_raw` using a `nn.Dense` layer followed by `nn.gelu`. This `cpe_projected` signal is then element-wise added to `x_raw`, forming `x_additive_augmented`, which then feeds into the CNN.\n\n```python\nclass Simple1DCNNWithAdditiveCPE(nn.Module):\n    # ... (standard hparams, plus CPE hparams)\n    @nn.compact\n    def\
    \ __call__(self, x_raw, deterministic=True):\n        cpe_generator = CPE_Generator(\n            d_input=self.d_input, pos_embedding_dim=self.pos_embedding_dim,\n            context_window_size=self.context_window_size, max_overall_seq_len=self.max_overall_seq_len,\n            name='cpe_generator'\n        )\n        cpe_output = cpe_generator(x_raw)\n        cpe_projected = nn.Dense(self.d_input, name='cpe_proj_for_additive')(cpe_output)\n        cpe_projected = nn.gelu(cpe_projected)\n        x_additive_augmented = x_raw + cpe_projected\n        # Apply 1D CNN layers to x_additive_augmented\n        # ...\n        x = nn.Conv(features=self.hidden_dim_conv1, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME', name='conv1')(x_additive_augmented)\n        x = nn.relu(x)\n        x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate_conv)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n        x = nn.Conv(features=self.hidden_dim_conv2, kernel_size=(self.kernel_size,), padding='SAME', name='conv2')(x)\n        x =\
    \ nn.relu(x)\n        x = nn.Dropout(rate=self.dropout_rate_conv)(x, deterministic=deterministic)\n\n        x = jnp.mean(x, axis=1) # Global Average Pooling\n        x = nn.Dense(self.d_output)(x)\n        return x.squeeze(-1) if self.d_output == 1 else x\n```\n\n### 2.4 Experimental Protocol\n\nAll experiments were conducted using `BASE_SEED = 42` and `BATCH_SIZE = 64`. The `create_optimizer` function utilized `optax.adamw` with a `learning_rate` of 0.001 and `weight_decay` of 0.01.\n\n## 3. Results\n\n### 3.1 Simple 1D CNN Baseline and Initial CPE Integration\n\n| Run | Evaluation ID | Aggregate Score | APA_R2 | CRI-Off_Spearman | CRI-On_Spearman | PRS_R2 | MRL_R2 | Modif_AUC-ROC | ncRNA_Accuracy |\n|-----|---------------|-----------------|--------|------------------|-----------------|--------|--------|---------------|----------------|\n| Simple 1D CNN Baseline (Quaero I) | 694 | 0.4833 | 0.6406 | 0.0637 | 0.4077 | 0.3807 | 0.5407 | 0.7049 | 0.5969 |\n| Simple 1D CNN Baseline (Veritas\
    \ III Rep.) | 832 | 0.4853 | 0.6473 | 0.0623 | 0.4148 | 0.4180 | 0.5458 | 0.7087 | 0.6000 |\n| Simple 1D CNN + CPE (Concatenation) | 838 | **0.5794** | 0.8035 | **0.0706** | 0.4810 | 0.5327 | 0.5379 | 0.9499 | 0.6800 |\n\n**Discussion:**\nThe integration of CPE via input concatenation (ID 838) into the Simple 1D CNN baseline resulted in a **highly significant improvement** in aggregate score, from 0.4853 (baseline) to 0.5794. This represents a 19.4% relative increase and demonstrates CPE's strong efficacy for this intermediate-complexity architecture. CRI-Off Spearman also saw a notable boost.\n\n### 3.2 CPE Parameter Study: `pos_embedding_dim`\n\nThe `pos_embedding_dim` parameter was varied while keeping `context_window_size=3`.\n\n| Run | Evaluation ID | `pos_embedding_dim` | Aggregate Score | APA_R2 | CRI-Off_Spearman | CRI-On_Spearman | PRS_R2 | MRL_R2 | Modif_AUC-ROC | ncRNA_Accuracy |\n|-----|---------------|---------------------|-----------------|--------|------------------|-----------------|--------|--------|---------------|----------------|\n\
    | Simple 1D CNN + CPE (Baseline) | 838 | 16 | **0.5794** | 0.8035 | 0.0706 | 0.4810 | 0.5327 | 0.5379 | 0.9499 | 0.6800 |\n| pos_dim=8 | 848 | 8 | 0.5657 | 0.8032 | 0.0723 | 0.4656 | 0.5276 | 0.5501 | 0.9504 | 0.5908 |\n| pos_dim=32 | 861 | 32 | 0.5636 | 0.8027 | 0.0710 | 0.4296 | 0.5324 | 0.5388 | 0.9493 | 0.6215 |\n\n**Discussion:**\nThe parameter study for `pos_embedding_dim` indicates that **16 is the optimal value** among the tested range (8, 16, 32). Both smaller (8) and larger (32) embedding dimensions resulted in a slight decrease in aggregate performance. This suggests that a balanced dimensionality is crucial for effectively capturing and integrating context-aware positional information.\n\n### 3.3 CPE Parameter Study: `context_window_size`\n\nThe `context_window_size` parameter was varied while fixing `pos_embedding_dim=16` (the optimal value).\n\n| Run | Evaluation ID | `context_window_size` | Aggregate Score | APA_R2 | CRI-Off_Spearman | CRI-On_Spearman | PRS_R2 | MRL_R2\
    \ | Modif_AUC-ROC | ncRNA_Accuracy |\n|-----|---------------|-----------------------|-----------------|--------|------------------|-----------------|--------|--------|---------------|----------------|\n| Simple 1D CNN + CPE (Baseline) | 838 | 3 | **0.5794** | 0.8035 | 0.0706 | 0.4810 | 0.5327 | 0.5379 | 0.9499 | 0.6800 |\n| context_size=1 | 866 | 1 | 0.5785 | 0.8043 | 0.0703 | 0.4576 | 0.5261 | 0.5441 | 0.9499 | 0.6969 |\n| context_size=5 | 870 | 5 | 0.5728 | 0.8051 | 0.0743 | 0.4425 | 0.5452 | 0.5433 | 0.9498 | 0.6492 |\n\n**Discussion:**\nThe `context_window_size` study showed that **`3` or `1` are optimal**, with `context_window_size=3` achieving the highest aggregate score. `context_window_size=1` (where the embedding is modulated only by the current nucleotide's features) performed very similarly. A broader window of 5 led to a slight performance degradation.\n\n### 3.4 CPE Integration Method Comparison\n\nThe identified optimal CPE configuration (`pos_embedding_dim=16`, `context_window_size=3`)\
    \ was used to compare input concatenation vs. additive fusion.\n\n| Run | Evaluation ID | Integration Method | Aggregate Score | APA_R2 | CRI-Off_Spearman | CRI-On_Spearman | PRS_R2 | MRL_R2 | Modif_AUC-ROC | ncRNA_Accuracy |\n|-----|---------------|--------------------|-----------------|--------|------------------|-----------------|--------|--------|---------------|----------------|\n| Input Concatenation | 838 | Concatenation | **0.5794** | 0.8035 | 0.0706 | 0.4810 | 0.5327 | 0.5379 | 0.9499 | 0.6800 |\n| Additive Fusion | 873 | Additive | 0.5533 | 0.7960 | 0.0770 | 0.4534 | 0.5280 | 0.5443 | 0.9500 | 0.5246 |\n\n**Discussion:**\nInput concatenation (ID 838) proved more effective than additive fusion (ID 873), yielding a higher aggregate score. This suggests that for the Simple 1D CNN, explicitly expanding the input feature space with CPE via concatenation is more beneficial. The additive approach, while not increasing input dimensionality, may not provide the same richness of combined\
    \ features that a larger input dimension offers to the convolutional layers. Notably, additive fusion (ID 873) showed a slightly higher CRI-Off score, indicating potential task-specific benefits that warrant further investigation, but not an overall improvement.\n\n## 4. Conclusion and Future Work\n\nThis systematic study robustly validates Contextual Position Embeddings (CPE) as a highly effective foundational mechanism for enhancing Simple 1D CNN architectures in Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling. CPE significantly improved the baseline's performance, with optimal parameters identified as `pos_embedding_dim=16` and `context_window_size=3` (or 1). Input concatenation emerged as the most effective integration method compared to additive fusion. This work reinforces CPE's value for architectures that benefit from explicit positional cues and provides concrete guidance for its implementation.\n\nFuture work includes:\n1.  **Exploration of CPE with Different CNN Architectures:** Testing\
    \ CPE on 1D CNNs with varying depths, kernel sizes, or specific residual connections to understand its interaction with a broader range of convolutional features.\n2.  **Adaptive CPE Generation:** Investigating mechanisms where `pos_embedding_dim` or `context_window_size` could be dynamically learned or adapted per-task, rather than fixed globally.\n3.  **Refined Additive Fusion:** Further optimizing the projection and activation of CPE for additive fusion, potentially through non-linear projections or learned weighting, especially given its slight CRI-Off benefit.\n4.  **Meta-Analysis with Other Positional Encodings:** A comparative study of CPE against other advanced positional encoding techniques (e.g., rotary embeddings, relative positional encoding) on various CNN baselines to further contextualize its strengths.\n5.  **Revisiting Deep Integration with Gating:** Re-evaluating deep integration of CPE into SOTA-level DSConv backbones with more sophisticated modulation mechanisms (e.g.,\
    \ learned gating or attention-based modulation), specifically addressing the interference observed in previous attempts (ID 815). This would follow the reviewer's latest suggestion from Archive ID 31.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_31-2**\nAuthor: Archive Review System (Tick 547)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**Reviewer Evaluation**\n\n**Score:** 9/10\n\n**Reviewer Comments:**\nThis is an outstanding submission and a model for how to respond to reviewer feedback. The agent has successfully addressed all previous critical flaws, transforming an unacceptable research log into a high-quality, focused, and fully reproducible scientific paper. The methodology is now exceptionally clear, and the systematic parameter and integration study provides valuable, concrete insights into the optimal use of Contextual Position Embeddings. This is a high-impact contribution that significantly advances the station's understanding of this novel technique.\n\n**Suggestions for Future Work:**\nThis paper provides\
    \ a strong, data-driven guide for using CPE in simpler CNNs. A powerful next step would be to apply these validated optimal parameters and the superior concatenation method to a more complex architecture, like the SOTA-level DSConv backbones. The key challenge, as hinted at in the agent's previous negative result (ID 815), is likely to be how to integrate the CPE signal without disrupting the features learned by the more sophisticated backbone. Exploring techniques like learned gating or attention to modulate the fusion of CPE with the backbone's internal representations could be a very fruitful research direction.\n\n*This evaluation was conducted by the automated review system to ensure publication quality standards.*\n### Message 2\n\nYour research submission 'Synergy Test: SOTA Backbone with Siamese Interaction Head for CRI-Off' (ID: 895) has been evaluated.\n\n**Score:** 0.6788156652504974\n**APA_R2:** 0.8536\n**CRI-Off_Spearman:** 0.0969\n**Modif_AUC-ROC:** 0.9508\n**CRI-On_Spearman:**\
    \ 0.4701\n**PRS_R2:** 0.5618\n**MRL_R2:** 0.8754\n**ncRNA_Accuracy:** 0.9431\n**Evaluation Details:** Evaluated on 7 datasets successfully\n\n**Full Execution Log:**\n```\nPYTHON SANDBOX EXECUTION:\nSTDOUT:\nExecuting command: CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES='' JAX_PLATFORMS='' python storage/system/main.py\n=== SIMPLE CPU VALIDATION ===\nValidating function loading and basic compatibility\n\nTesting APA configuration...\n✓ APA network creation works\n✓ APA network forward pass works - output shape: (4,)\n✓ APA optimizer creation works\n\nTesting CRI-Off configuration...\n✓ CRI-Off network creation works\n✓ CRI-Off network forward pass works - output shape: (4,)\n✓ CRI-Off optimizer creation works\n\nTesting Modif configuration...\n✓ Modif network creation works\n✓ Modif network forward pass works - output shape: (4, 12)\n✓ Modif optimizer creation works\n\nTesting CRI-On configuration...\n✓ CRI-On network creation works\n✓ CRI-On network forward pass works - output shape: (4,)\n✓ CRI-On optimizer\
    \ creation works\n\nTesting PRS configuration...\n✓ PRS network creation works\n✓ PRS network forward pass works - output shape: (4, 3)\n✓ PRS optimizer creation works\n\nTesting MRL configuration...\n✓ MRL network creation works\n✓ MRL network forward pass works - output shape: (4,)\n✓ MRL optimizer creation works\n\nTesting ncRNA configuration...\n✓ ncRNA network creation works\n✓ ncRNA network forward pass works - output shape: (4, 13)\n✓ ncRNA optimizer creation works\nValidation successful - all functions work correctly for all datasets!\n\nStarting full Ray multi-dataset training...\n=== FULL RAY TRAINING MODE ===\nConnecting to Ray cluster at 10.60.151.21:6379\nRay cluster initialized successfully\nAvailable resources: 896 CPUs, 72 GPUs\n\nFunction sources:\n  _define_hyperparameters: from submission\n  create_network: from submission\n  compute_loss: HARDCODED (agents cannot override)\n  create_optimizer: from submission\n  complete: using default\n  BASE_SEED: 545957 (using\
    \ time-based default\n  BATCH_SIZE: 64 (using default)\n============================================================\n============================================================\nStarting Ray Tune RNA Sequence Modeling (MULTI-DATASET RNA TRAINING)\nConfiguration: 7 datasets (APA, CRI-Off, Modif, CRI-On, PRS, MRL, ncRNA) for multi-task evaluation\nParallel trials: 7, Total trials: 7\nTimeout: 30.0 min per trial, 240.0 min total\n============================================================\n\nStarting Ray Tune multi-dataset training...\n\nStarting RNA training on CRI-On dataset\nTask type: regression, Output dim: 1\nMetric: spearman, Timeout: 30.0min\n\nCRI-On Training (regression, spearman)\nData: 1453 train, 207 val, seq_len=23\n=============================================\nEpoch|  Loss  |Val SPEARMAN|  Best  \n=============================================\n 10  | 0.0124 |  0.2833  | 0.2833 \n 20  | 0.0106 |  0.3969  | 0.3969 \n 30  | 0.0057 |  0.4701  | 0.4701 \n 40  | 0.0013 |  0.4320\
    \  | 0.4701 \n=============================================\nEarly stop at epoch 40 (no improvement for 10 epochs)\n=============================================\nBest:0.4701 Time:24s\n\nCRI-On training completed:\nValidation spearman: 0.470078\nTraining time: 31s\n\nStarting RNA training on CRI-Off dataset\nTask type: regression, Output dim: 1\nMetric: spearman, Timeout: 30.0min\n\nCRI-Off Training (regression, spearman)\nData: 14223 train, 2032 val, seq_len=46\n=============================================\nEpoch|  Loss  |Val SPEARMAN|  Best  \n=============================================\n 10  | 0.0004 |  0.0908  | 0.0908 \n 20  | 0.0002 |  0.0961  | 0.0969 \n=============================================\nEarly stop at epoch 26 (no improvement for 10 epochs)\n=============================================\nBest:0.0969 Time:186s\n\nCRI-Off training completed:\nValidation spearman: 0.096860\nTraining time: 195s\n\nStarting RNA training on MRL dataset\nTask type: regression, Output dim:\
    \ 1\nMetric: r2, Timeout: 30.0min\n\nMRL Training (regression, r2)\nData: 76319 train, 7600 val, seq_len=100\n=============================================\nEpoch|  Loss  |  Val R2  |  Best  \n=============================================\n 10  | 0.1231 |  0.8740  | 0.8740 \n 20  | 0.0799 |  0.8616  | 0.8754 \n=============================================\nEarly stop at epoch 25 (no improvement for 10 epochs)\n=============================================\nBest:0.8754 Time:932s\n\nMRL training completed:\nValidation r2: 0.875438\nTraining time: 941s\n\nStarting RNA training on ncRNA dataset\nTask type: multiclass_classification, Output dim: 13\nMetric: accuracy, Timeout: 30.0min\n\nncRNA Training (multiclass_classification, accuracy)\nData: 5670 train, 650 val, seq_len=1136\n=============================================\nEpoch|  Loss  |Val ACCURACY|  Best  \n=============================================\n 10  | 0.2546 |  0.9077  | 0.9077 \n 20  | 0.0323 |  0.9215  | 0.9431 \n=============================================\n\
    Early stop at epoch 25 (no improvement for 10 epochs)\n=============================================\nBest:0.9431 Time:945s\n\nncRNA training completed:\nValidation accuracy: 0.943077\nTraining time: 953s\n\nStarting RNA training on PRS dataset\nTask type: multilabel_regression, Output dim: 3\nMetric: r2, Timeout: 30.0min\n\nPRS Training (multilabel_regression, r2)\nData: 73227 train, 9153 val, seq_len=148\n=============================================\nEpoch|  Loss  |  Val R2  |  Best  \n=============================================\n 10  | 0.0332 |  0.5515  | 0.5618 \n=============================================\nEarly stop at epoch 19 (no improvement for 10 epochs)\n=============================================\nBest:0.5618 Time:1084s\n\nPRS training completed:\nValidation r2: 0.561809\nTraining time: 1094s\n\nStarting RNA training on Modif dataset\nTask type: multilabel_classification, Output dim: 12\nMetric: auc_roc, Timeout: 30.0min\n\nModif Training (multilabel_classification,\
    \ auc_roc)\nData: 304661 train, 3599 val, seq_len=101\n=============================================\nEpoch|  Loss  |Val AUC_ROC|  Best  \n=============================================\n 10  | 0.0631 |  0.9481  | 0.9508 \n=============================================\nTimeout epoch 13\n=============================================\nBest:0.9508 Time:1813s\n\nModif training completed:\nValidation auc_roc: 0.950830\nTraining time: 1829s\n\nStarting RNA training on APA dataset\nTask type: regression, Output dim: 1\nMetric: r2, Timeout: 30.0min\n\nAPA Training (regression, r2)\nData: 145463 train, 33170 val, seq_len=186\n=============================================\nEpoch|  Loss  |  Val R2  |  Best  \n=============================================\n 10  | 0.0116 |  0.8509  | 0.8527 \n=============================================\nTimeout epoch 14\n=============================================\nBest:0.8536 Time:1920s\n\nAPA training completed:\nValidation r2: 0.853618\nTraining time: 1935s\n\
