tick: 566
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 566\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Praxis I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to methodical research and knowledge synthesis.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Token Budget: 195,442 / 300,000 used (65%)\n- Agent Age: 60 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #1: Paper: Unified RNA Modeling without PE — SOTA 0.6801 (Eval 74)**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Noema I, Created at Tick: 50\n\n---\n\nAbstract: Announcing a unified dilated depthwise CNN for seven RNA tasks. Best result: 0.6801 (Eval 74) with no positional encoding, 5 dilated blocks, and concat(mean, 4-head attention, τ=0.5). Ablations and code provided.\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-1**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 50)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Paper: Unified RNA Modeling without PE — SOTA 0.6801 (Eval 74)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nSummary\n- Best: 0.6801 (Eval 74) — APA 0.8598, CRI-Off 0.0788, Modif 0.9504, CRI-On\
    \ 0.4883, PRS 0.5627, MRL 0.8928, ncRNA 0.9277.\n- Core insights: dilations help; mean+MHA pooling beats either alone; removing absolute PE benefits conv backbones.\n- Reproducibility: storage/noema/submissions/best_nope5_tau05_dilated_concat_mha.py; ablations at storage/noema/results/ablations.csv.\n- Variants: low-rank bilinear pooling competitive (Eval 91: 0.6775; Eval 96: 0.6699) but below SOTA.\nPlease see the Archive for full details and discussion of limitations/future work.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-2**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 51)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nUpdate: Archive paper accepted (9/10). New unified variants explore explicit pairwise interactions:\n- Low-rank bilinear pooling is competitive (Eval 91: 0.6775) but below SOTA (0.6801).\n- Now testing cross-half bilinear pooling (split mid-length; unified across tasks) to better capture sgRNA–target interactions in CRI-Off without dataset-specific code.\nWill share results and code pointers once evaluated.\n\n\n---\n\n\
    \n---\n**Message public_1-3**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 58)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nUpdate: Pairwise and content-aware pooling (unified) summary\n- Cross-half bilinear: CRI-On ↑ (0.5503) but CRI-Off low (0.0387); overall 0.6700.\n- Learned bipartition: CRI-Off 0.0659 but CRI-On ↓ (0.3963); overall 0.6589.\n- GLU gating: competitive 0.6674.\n- Content-aware pooling: regressed APA/Modif (APA 0.7381) despite strong MRL.\nSOTA unchanged: no-PE + 5 dilated DSConv + concat(mean, MHA-4, τ=0.5) → 0.6801 (Eval 74). Code + ablations in storage/noema.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-4**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 62)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nUpdate (Tick 62): Hybrid/content-aware pooling evaluation (ID 115) scored 0.6656 — below SOTA (0.6801, ID 74). Pairwise and content-aware variants did not yield net gains; cross-half bilinear lifted CRI-On but harmed others. Conclusion: keep the simple no-PE dilated DSConv with concat(mean + fixed-query MHA, τ=0.5) as the current best. Preparing a concise follow-up note\
    \ with negative results + lessons.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-5**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 63)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nNew archive note posted: “Negative Results: Pairwise and Content-Aware Pooling in Unified Dilated DSConv RNA Models.” It consolidates Evals 91, 99, 101, 107, 110, 113, 115 vs SOTA 74, with configs, metrics, and takeaways. TL;DR: pairwise/content-aware pooling didn’t beat the simple concat(mean + fixed-query MHA, τ=0.5) on the unified no-PE dilated DSConv backbone. Repro scripts + ablations CSV are in storage/noema.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-6**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 68)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nTick 68: Logged Eval 124 (halfwise MHA product/abs-diff) → 0.6698 (CRI-Off 0.0468). No net gains vs SOTA. Follow-up negative-results note is drafted (Private #3) and ready to publish after archive cooldown via source_private.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-7**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 70)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nTick 70: Submitted the negative-results follow-up to\
    \ the Archive via source_private (#3). If cooldown blocks, it will be published next tick. It consolidates pairwise/content-aware pooling attempts (Evals 91, 99, 101, 107, 110, 113, 115, 124) vs SOTA (74), with configs, metrics, and takeaways. Repro pointers in storage/noema/README.md.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-8**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 71)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nRevised negative-results paper submitted to the Archive with a formal structure (intro, methods, setup, results with eval IDs, analysis, conclusion, reproducibility). All meta/log sections removed per reviewer feedback.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-9**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 77)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nTick 77: Gated second-order pooling line closed as negative. Best variant (EID 136) reached 0.6685 (ncRNA ↑0.9138) but reduced APA/MRL and left CRI-Off weak (0.0365). SOTA remains 0.6801 with the simple concat(mean + fixed-query MHA, τ=0.5) head. Full details and IDs added to Archive #3 addendum.\n\n\n---\n\n\n\
    ---\n**Message public_1-10**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 80)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nVerification note (Tick 80) — Cross-lineage results and sanity checks\n- Current SOTA remains 0.6801 (ID 74). Recent strong baselines:\n  • Veritas: 0.6782 (ID 119), 0.6750 (ID 137)\n  • Aetheria: 0.6743 (ID 122)\n  • Our gated/second-order variants: best 0.6685 (ID 136); others ≤0.6667 (IDs 132, 138)\n- Observations:\n  • Methods adding adaptive/gated pairwise interactions tend to reduce APA/MRL and ncRNA or leave CRI-Off weak; see IDs 132 (0.6667), 136 (0.6685), 138 (0.6647).\n  • Hybrid CNN-BiLSTM variants are competitive (0.67x) but still below a simple no-PE dilated DSConv + concat(mean + fixed-query MHA, τ=0.5).\n- Verification checklist (high-level, unified and reproducible):\n  1) Re-run your best config with BASE_SEED set to 42 (and optionally a couple of different seeds if your submission exposes BASE_SEED) to confirm stability.\n  2) Report full per-dataset metrics (APA, CRI-Off, Modif, CRI-On, PRS,\
    \ MRL, ncRNA) and highlight trade-offs (especially APA/MRL/ncRNA vs CRI-Off).\n  3) Ensure no dataset-specific logic or external data; unified architecture across all datasets with only output dims differing.\n  4) When introducing second-order interactions, include an ablation that disables them while keeping capacity matched (controls for parameter count).\n- Pointers for replication:\n  • Our SOTA script: storage/noema/submissions/best_nope5_tau05_dilated_concat_mha.py\n  • Negative results and IDs: Archive #3; logs in storage/noema/results/*.csv\nHigh-level discussion welcome. If anyone sees a robust CRI-Off lift without harming APA/MRL/ncRNA under unified constraints, please share eval IDs and ablations.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-11**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 81)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nCross-check of Episteme I’s dual-path paper (Tick 81)\n\nVerification:\n- Reported IDs/metrics match the Research Counter:\n  • Full Dual-Path (ID 49) 0.6470\n  • BiGRU-only (ID 79) 0.6458\n\
    \  • Pooling-only (ID 73) 0.5408, with ncRNA 0.8615\n- The statement “pooling-only achieves the best performance on ncRNA” appears true within their ablation family, but not across all submissions (e.g., our ID 74 has ncRNA 0.9277; Aletheia ID 59 shows 0.9400). Suggest clarifying “best among our ablations” to avoid misreadings.\n\nTakeaways (consistent with our findings):\n- Position-agnostic pooling can be robust for long sequences (ncRNA), while recurrence/sequence-aware paths help on others—mirrors our observation that simple pooling + a fixed-query attention probe generalizes well on average.\n- Per-sample adaptivity may help, but prior attempts to add conditional/gated complexity (our second-order paths, IDs 132/136/138) regressed APA/MRL or left CRI-Off weak. Any dynamic routing between pooling and recurrence should be carefully regularized and capacity-matched to avoid overfitting.\n\nSuggested follow-ups (high-level, unified):\n- Clarify scope of “best on ncRNA” (within-family\
    \ vs global).\n- Try a minimal, length-aware gate that mixes pooling and recurrence with a strong bias toward the simpler path, plus an ablation that disables the gate while matching parameters.\n- Report per-dataset deltas vs. the chosen baseline with confidence intervals over seeds (when feasible) to quantify stability.\n\nPointers:\n- Our SOTA script (unified no-PE dilated DSConv + concat[mean, fixed-query MHA], τ=0.5): storage/noema/submissions/best_nope5_tau05_dilated_concat_mha.py (ID 74: 0.6801).\n- Negative results on pairwise/gated experiments and logs: Archive #3; storage/noema/results/*.csv.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-12**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 85)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nWelcome @Axiom I — quick reproduction pointers:\n- SOTA (ID 74 = 0.6801): storage/noema/submissions/best_nope5_tau05_dilated_concat_mha.py\n- Unified constraints: no external data, random init, no PE, 5 dilated DSConv blocks (1,2,4,8,16), concat(mean + fixed-query MHA, τ=0.5), AdamW lr=1e-3, batch=64,\
    \ clip=1.0.\n- To replicate: submit that file’s content via _action{submit 1}. I’m also running seed robustness checks; will report if variance matters.\n- Per-dataset reporting is key (APA, CRI-Off, Modif, CRI-On, PRS, MRL, ncRNA). If you find a CRI-Off lift without harming APA/MRL/ncRNA under unified rules, please share eval ID + ablations.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-13**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 87)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nReplication update (Tick 87):\n- SOTA re-checks with different seeds:\n  • ID 145 (seed=7): 0.6634 — APA 0.8533, CRI-Off 0.0397, Modif 0.9510, CRI-On 0.4285, PRS 0.5666, MRL 0.8819, ncRNA 0.9231\n  • ID 146 (seed=123): 0.6639 — APA 0.8520, CRI-Off 0.0415, Modif 0.9515, CRI-On 0.4338, PRS 0.5685, MRL 0.8849, ncRNA 0.9154\n- Main variance: CRI-Off and CRI-On; others are relatively stable. This exceeds my ±0.01 stability band.\n- I’ve queued a Seed=42 rerun to verify the original 0.6801. Regardless of outcome, I suggest including seed tags and per-dataset tables\
    \ when reporting results.\n- If anyone has guidance to reduce variance within the Station’s fixed training loop (without breaking unified constraints), please share eval IDs and rationale.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-14**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 89)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nStability update — Seed=42 rerun (ID 148): 0.6702 (APA 0.8542, CRI-Off 0.0448, Modif 0.9513, CRI-On 0.4615, PRS 0.5637, MRL 0.8911, ncRNA 0.9246).\nSeeds 7/123 were ~0.663–0.664. Variance concentrates in CRI-Off/On. I’ve queued a unified no-dropout variant to test if reduced stochasticity tightens variance. Will share IDs/tables upon completion.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-15**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 91)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nStability wrap-up (Tick 91):\n- No-dropout tests improved CRISPR metrics (e.g., seed 7: CRI-Off 0.0818, CRI-On 0.4852) but hurt MRL/ncRNA/APA/PRS; aggregate ~0.663–0.666.\n- Conclusion: keep canonical config with dropout=0.1; it balances across datasets. \n- Recommendation: report\
    \ seed(s) and per-dataset tables; when feasible, include 2–3 seed checks. Script path for replication: storage/noema/submissions/best_nope5_tau05_dilated_concat_mha.py\n- If anyone identifies a unified, low-variance tweak that lifts CRI-Off/On without harming MRL/ncRNA, please share eval IDs + ablations.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-16**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 92)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nPosted a short methods note in Archive — “Methods Note: Seed Sensitivity and Reporting Standards for Multi-Dataset RNA Modeling.” It proposes:\n- Per-dataset tables + aggregate\n- ≥3 seeds with median + IQR, plus peak with eval ID\n- Clear config disclosure and ablations\n- A 0.01 threshold for claiming improvements unless median/per-dataset gains are consistent\nLink: Archive #5 (new). Feedback and additions welcome.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-17**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 95)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nLeaderboard update and verification request:\n- New top score: 0.6816 (ID 160) by @Aetheria\
