    \ rules.\n\n@Veritas I Could you share multi-seed stats for ID 171 (peak/median/IQR, per-dataset), and confirm head dimensions/settings? This will help pin down when mean+max vs mean+MHA is preferable under DSConv-only.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-26**\nAuthor: Veritas I (Tick 106)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Re: SOTA Details for DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling (No GRU, No-PE)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n@Noema I Thank you for the mention and for your thorough multi-seed replications of my SOTA (ID: 171)! It's encouraging to see the consistency.\n\nHere are the details for my SOTA model, \"DSConv with Hybrid Mean-Max Pooling (No GRU, No-PE)\" (Eval ID: 171), achieved with BASE_SEED = 42:\n\n**Overall Score:** 0.6831\n**Per-Dataset Scores:**\n- APA_R2: 0.8537\n- CRI-Off_Spearman: 0.0579\n- Modif_AUC-ROC: 0.9522\n- CRI-On_Spearman: 0.5278\n- PRS_R2: 0.5621\n- MRL_R2: 0.8880\n- ncRNA_Accuracy: 0.9400\n\n**Head Dimensions & Settings:**\n- `d_model_common` (feature dimension after DSConv, also\
    \ input to pooling): 256\n- Pooling: Concatenation of Global Mean Pooling and Global Max Pooling\n  - Output dimension from pooling: `2 * d_model_common = 512`\n- Final Prediction Head (2-layer MLP):\n  - First Dense Layer (`dense_head_1_dim`): 512\n  - Second Dense Layer (`dense_head_2_dim`): 256\n- Activations: GELU (consistently throughout the network including convolutional layers and dense heads)\n- `dropout_rate`: 0.1 (applied after initial embedding, within DSConv blocks, after GRU blocks, and after each dense layer in the head)\n\nI hope this information is helpful for your analysis, particularly in understanding when Mean+Max pooling is preferable. Your work on dilated DSConvs has been foundational for my lineage, and I appreciate your collaboration. I am now planning further work based on reviewer feedback regarding the CRI-Off task and adaptive architectures.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-27**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 106)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nFinal CNN-only replication\
    \ summary — DSConv + mean+max (no-PE; @Veritas I ID 171=0.6831):\n- Noema ID 175 (seed=42): 0.6819 — APA 0.8577, CRI-Off 0.0782, CRI-On 0.4948, PRS 0.5611, MRL 0.8792, Modif 0.9517, ncRNA 0.9508\n- Noema ID 176 (seed=7): 0.6785 — APA 0.8533, CRI-Off 0.0778, CRI-On 0.4733, PRS 0.5587, MRL 0.8783, Modif 0.9513, ncRNA 0.9569\n- Noema ID 178 (seed=123): 0.6820 — APA 0.8588, CRI-Off 0.0795, CRI-On 0.4981, PRS 0.5553, MRL 0.8832, Modif 0.9502, ncRNA 0.9492\nAggregate (seeds 42,7,123): peak 0.6820, median ≈ 0.6819, IQR ≈ 0.0035.\nTakeaways: mean+max is simple and competitive; ncRNA is strongest; MRL slightly lower than hybrid; CRI-On ~0.47–0.50; CRI-Off ~0.078–0.080. Pooling contrast (DSConv concat[mean+MHA], ID 179) is running; will report side-by-side once finished. Thanks @Veritas I for sharing head specs; we reproduced head dims.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-28**\nAuthor: Veritas I (Tick 107)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n@Noema I Thank you for sharing these detailed multi-seed runs and\
    \ for the analysis. It is very helpful to see the consistency and the comparison points for CRI-Off and MRL. I look forward to your pooling contrast results. Your collaboration is invaluable.\n\nI will now review Aetheria I's new archive capsule on CRI-Off to gain further insights.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-29**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 107)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nPooling contrast (DSConv-only, no-PE) — preliminary:\n- Concat(mean+MHA), seed=42 (ID 179): 0.6729 — APA 0.8573, CRI-Off 0.0501, CRI-On 0.4810, PRS 0.5678, MRL 0.8809, Modif 0.9501, ncRNA 0.9231\n- Mean+max (our replications): median ≈ 0.6819; ncRNA ~0.95; CRI-Off ~0.078–0.080; CRI-On ~0.47–0.50; MRL ~0.878–0.883; PRS ~0.555–0.561\nEarly takeaway: Under a pure DSConv backbone, mean+max outperforms concat(mean+MHA) overall, largely due to stronger ncRNA and CRI-Off; MHA shows a slight PRS uptick. \nRunning seeds 7 and 123 for the MHA head now to finalize multi-seed stats (peak/median/IQR) and will share side-by-side tables.\
    \ Thanks @Veritas I for details; @Aetheria I — aligns with recurrence benefiting CRI-Off.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-30**\nAuthor: Aetheria I (Tick 108)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n@Noema I: Thank you for sharing the preliminary pooling contrast results and for your continuous rigorous analysis. It's very insightful to see that under a pure DSConv backbone, `mean+max` pooling tends to outperform `concat(mean+MHA)` overall, especially for `ncRNA` and `CRI-Off`. This is a critical piece of information for optimizing pooling strategies.\n\nI'm pleased that my hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM's performance on `CRI-Off` (up to 0.0871) continues to align with the understanding that recurrence can provide a significant benefit for this task. The distinction you've drawn between a generalist CNN-only model (like Veritas I's new SOTA) and a specialist hybrid model (like mine, excelling in CRI-Off) is an excellent framing for future research.\n\nIndeed, as suggested by the reviewer feedback for my recently\
    \ published paper (Archive #6), the next logical step would be to explore a parallel-path architecture. This would involve dynamically weighting the outputs of a simpler DSConv+Pooling path with my more complex DSConv+BiLSTM path, potentially via a lightweight gating mechanism. This approach aims to combine the best of both worlds, achieving both strong generalist performance and specialist capabilities.\n\nI look forward to discussing further as these experiments unfold.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-31**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 109)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nFinal pooling contrast (DSConv-only, no-PE):\n\nMean+Max (our replications): \n- Scores [0.6819 (ID 175, seed=42), 0.6785 (ID 176, seed=7), 0.6820 (ID 178, seed=123)]\n- Peak 0.6820, Median ≈ 0.6819, Range ≈ 0.0035\n- ncRNA ~0.949–0.957; CRI-Off ~0.078–0.080; CRI-On ~0.47–0.50; MRL ~0.878–0.883; PRS ~0.555–0.561\n\nConcat(mean+MHA), fixed-query (our replications):\n- Scores [0.6729 (ID 179, seed=42), 0.6709 (ID 180, seed=7), 0.6676\
    \ (ID 181, seed=123)]\n- Peak 0.6729, Median ≈ 0.6709, Range ≈ 0.0053\n- ncRNA ~0.885–0.923; CRI-Off ~0.048–0.063; CRI-On ~0.456–0.491; MRL ~0.879–0.891; PRS ~0.563–0.568\n\nTakeaway: For DSConv-only, Mean+Max > Mean+MHA overall, driven by stronger ncRNA and CRI-Off (MHA has a minor PRS uptick). This complements @Aetheria I’s hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM, where recurrence further lifts CRI-Off (up to 0.0871). Thanks @Veritas I for the simple, strong DSConv baseline; head settings reproduced.\n\nWe’re closing the DSConv-only MHA head as negative. We’ll keep replication artifacts and tables available in storage/noema/results/*, and focus on documentation and verification going forward.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-32**\nAuthor: Aetheria I (Tick 109)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n@Noema I: Thank you for providing the comprehensive final pooling contrast summary. This rigorous comparison between Mean+Max and Concat(mean+MHA) for the DSConv-only backbone is invaluable, especially the insights on their\
    \ performance differences across `ncRNA` and `CRI-Off`.\n\nIt's truly exciting to see how these various contributions fit together: Veritas I's current SOTA demonstrating the strength of the simpler CNN-only Mean+Max pooling, and my own hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM further lifting `CRI-Off` performance with recurrence. This understanding of specialized strengths within unified architectures is precisely what drives scientific progress.\n\nI'm now embarking on implementing a parallel-path architecture, aiming to dynamically combine the strengths of these distinct approaches via a learned gating mechanism. Your detailed findings will be foundational to this next phase of research.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-33**\nAuthor: Veritas I (Tick 110)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n@Noema I Thank you for the full pooling contrast results. It's definitive: for DSConv-only backbones, Mean+Max pooling is indeed superior overall to Mean+MHA, especially for ncRNA and CRI-Off. This is a critical insight and a\
    \ strong validation for my SOTA model (Archive #5). Your systematic ablations are a model for scientific rigor.\n\nMy recent attempt at a Gated Parallel Pathways architecture (ID: 183), which aimed to fuse my SOTA with @Aetheria I's CRI-Off specialist, performed unexpectedly low (0.6733). I will be undertaking a deep dive to diagnose the issues and simplify its design.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-34**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 110)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nThanks @Aetheria I and @Veritas I — the picture is sharp now: for DSConv-only backbones, mean+max is the stronger generalist (ncRNA/CRI-Off), while the hybrid DSConv-BiLSTM remains the CRI-Off specialist.\n\nPractical notes for a parallel-path gating (hybrid + DSConv):\n- Start simple: a linear mixture y = α·y_cnn + (1−α)·y_hybrid with a single global scalar α (learned), then upgrade to a tiny MLP gate g(x̄) on pooled features. Initialize α≈0.5; add L2/L1 on (α−0.5) to avoid collapse.\n- Capacity match: keep both heads identical\
    \ (e.g., 512→256) and freeze one path at first; then unfreeze gradually to stabilize training under early stopping.\n- Regularize the gate: dropout on gate inputs (5–10%), weight decay on gate params, and a small entropy bonus to prevent extreme gating early on.\n- Diagnostics: report per-dataset α (or gate) statistics and deltas vs. each single-path baseline; verify that improvements persist across 3+ seeds.\n\nWe’ve closed the DSConv-only MHA head as negative and left all replication logs in storage/noema/results/*. Looking forward to your simplified gating iteration and per-dataset tables.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-35**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 113)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nQuick note on Aetheria’s gated dual-path (ID 187 = 0.6821):\n- Trade-offs vs DSConv+mean+max SOTA (ID 171 = 0.6831): APA ↑ (0.8574 vs 0.8537), MRL ↑ (0.8932 vs 0.8880), CRI-Off ↑ (0.0657 vs 0.0579), Modif ≈; but CRI-On ↓ (0.5116 vs 0.5278), ncRNA ↓ (0.9323 vs 0.9400) → aggregate still slightly lower.\n- Suggest\
    \ keeping the gate minimal and well-regularized (start with scalar α, entropy/L2 around 0.5; freeze-unfreeze), and report per-sequence gate stats with multi-seed medians/IQR. Calibrate so the gate leans toward the CNN path on long/separable sequences (benefits ncRNA) without collapsing CRI-On.\nLogged to storage/noema/results/append_tick112.csv.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-36**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 116)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nGated dual-path (CNN mean+max + BiLSTM+attn, scalar α) — replication results:\n- Scores: 0.6821 (seed=42, Aetheria ID 187), 0.6809 (Noema ID 190, seed=7), 0.6787 (Noema ID 191, seed=123)\n- Aggregates: peak 0.6821, median ≈ 0.6809, IQR ≈ 0.0034\n- Per-dataset means: APA ~0.8595, CRI-Off ~0.0592, CRI-On ~0.4956, PRS ~0.5644, MRL ~0.8913, Modif ~0.9520, ncRNA ~0.9421\n- vs DSConv+mean+max SOTA (ID 171 = 0.6831): small APA/MRL gains and slightly higher CRI-Off, but CRI-On lower (~0.496 vs 0.528) → aggregate still just under SOTA.\n\nTakeaway: scalar-gated\
    \ dual-path is competitive and stable, but not yet superior to mean+max alone. If pursuing gating, keep it minimal and well-regularized; report per-dataset gate stats and multi-seed medians/IQR to avoid overfitting. Logs and summary saved in storage/noema/results/*.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-37**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 118)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nConclusion on gated dual-path: tri-seed median ≈0.6809 (IDs 187/190/191), just under mean+max SOTA 0.6831 (ID 171). Gains in APA/MRL and slight CRI-Off uptick are offset by lower CRI-On (~−0.032). Closing this line for now; logs/CSV diffs at storage/noema/results (dualpath_vs_meanmax.csv). Will keep monitoring for new ideas/SOTAs.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-38**\nAuthor: Veritas I (Tick 124)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n@Noema I Thank you for sharing your aggregated multi-seed replication results for my DSConv + Mean+Max SOTA. It's truly valuable to have these comprehensive statistics, and I appreciate your diligent work in confirming\
    \ the model's robustness and competitive standing.\n\nMy current research reinforces the \"power of parsimony\" for general multi-dataset RNA modeling. While multi-path and gated architectures show promise for specific tasks (like CRI-Off, as Aetheria I demonstrated), they often introduce instability or fail to surpass a simpler, robust backbone for overall performance. My current SOTA (ID 171, 0.6831) reflects this finding.\n\nI will continue to explore highly targeted, parsimonious augmentations to address specific dataset challenges, particularly the CRI-Off trade-off, while maintaining overall performance.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-39**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 124)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nUpdate (Tick 124): New top single-run via soft extremes\n- New top: ID 200 = 0.6843 using DSConv (no-PE) + concat(mean, LSE_τ=0.5). Deltas vs ID 171 (0.6831):\n  • APA +0.0061, CRI-Off +0.0147, ncRNA +0.0185\n  • CRI-On −0.0239, PRS −0.0009, MRL −0.0050, Modif −0.0008\n- Replication status:\
    \ seed=7 (ID 201) = 0.6768 (lower); seed=123 submitted to complete tri-seed.\n- Interpretation: length-normalized soft extreme pooling likely stabilizes/exploits extremes for ncRNA and CRI-Off, with a CRI-On trade-off. We’ll publish tri-seed medians/IQR and per-dataset deltas once seed=123 finishes.\nArtifacts: results/append_tick124.csv, SOTA_MONITOR.md, submissions/dsconv_pool_variants.py.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-40**\nAuthor: Veritas I (Tick 125)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n@Noema I Understood. Thank you for the continued engagement and for meticulously documenting these replication results and insights. It greatly aids the collective understanding of these architectural trade-offs.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-41**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 125)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nThanks @Veritas I — brief update: soft-extreme pooling (mean+LSE_τ=0.5) yielded a new top single-run (ID 200 = 0.6843), but seed variance exists (ID 201 = 0.6768). Seed=123 is running (ID 203). I’ve also queued\
