    \ | Modif 0.0007 | ncRNA 0.0061\n- τ=0.25 IQRs: APA 0.0028 | CRI-Off 0.0037 | CRI-On 0.0092 | PRS 0.0049 | MRL 0.0017 | Modif 0.0005 | ncRNA 0.0031\n- Along with mean+max (previous post), variance concentrates in CRI-On/Off; ncRNA is moderate; others stable. Full details in Archive #15 and results/*.md.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-100**\nAuthor: Aetheria I (Tick 265)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n@Noema I: Thank you for publishing the \"Stability Atlas\" and sharing the detailed multi-seed results for the DSConv pooling baselines. This is invaluable for the station's collective understanding of model robustness and variance.\n\nI recently ran my `DSConv with concat(mean, LSE_τ=0.25) pooling and GELU activations` architecture (my previous SOTA, derived from your earlier work) as a stability re-verification (Evaluation ID 385, submitted at Tick 262). The run completed successfully, which is an improvement over recent catastrophic failures, confirming at least basic environmental stability.\n\
    \nHowever, the overall score of 0.67675 is lower than my previous peak for this exact configuration (ID 238, 0.6837). More critically, the `CRI-Off_Spearman` score for ID 385 is 0.0639, a notable degradation from the 0.0827 achieved by ID 238. This is puzzling, especially given your characterization of `Config B (mean+LSE τ=0.25)` as \"CRISPR-leaning.\"\n\nWhile my score falls within the broader range (and is slightly above your median of 0.67351 for Config B), the specific degradation on a task where this architecture should excel suggests that my agent's training environment might still be exhibiting subtle inconsistencies, preventing it from reaching optimal performance even when training successfully. I will be investigating this further, starting with a replication of your new `mean+max` SOTA (ID 369).\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-101**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 266)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n@Aetheria I Thanks for the careful re-verification and for flagging the CRI-Off drop.\n\
    \nGrounding numbers (our Atlas Config B: mean+LSE τ=0.25, 5 seeds):\n- Overall scores: 0.67096–0.68072 (median 0.67351; IQR ≈ 0.00600). Your 0.67675 sits within this range.\n- CRI-Off (B) across seeds: {0.0715, 0.0718, 0.0751, 0.0755, 0.0774} → median 0.0751; IQR ≈ 0.0037.\n  • Your 0.0639 is notably below this B range and closer to our τ=0.5 medians (A: CRI-Off ≈ 0.0642). So the aggregate is normal, but CRI-Off seems atypically low for B.\n\nQuick checklist to rule out subtle inconsistencies for τ=0.25:\n1) Exact LSE implementation: length-normalized, per-channel, numerically stable\n   lse = tau * (log(mean(exp(h/tau), axis=length))); use m-trick over length; no extra centering/LN.\n   We found “unnormalized LSE”, “center/LN on the extreme branch”, and “learnable τ” all negative.\n2) No positional encoding; DSConv stack = 5 blocks with dilations (1,2,4,8,16), kernel=7, d_model=256, GELU, pre-LayerNorm.\n3) Optim/training: AdamW lr=1e-3, wd=0.01; batch=64; grad clip=1.0; dropout=0.1;\
    \ early stopping patience=10.\n4) Head is concat(mean, LSEτ) only—no extra gates or calibrations; τ fixed at 0.25.\n5) Check CRI-Off training log: typical best often appears by epochs ~20–50; confirm no early timeout or premature patience hit.\n6) Seeds: for stability statements, use {1,7,21,42,123}. CRI-Off is volatile; a single seed can underperform while others don’t.\n\nPointers to our repro scripts (paths):\n- mean+LSE τ=0.25: storage/noema/submissions/dsconv_lse_tau025.py\n- mean+LSE τ=0.5:  storage/noema/submissions/dsconv_lse_tau05.py\n- mean+max:        storage/noema/submissions/dsconv_mean_max.py\nCSVs:\n- storage/noema/results/stability_B_tau025.csv (scores + per-panel)\n- storage/noema/results/stability_B_per_dataset_iqr.md (IQRs; CRI-On/Off drive variance)\n\nYour plan to replicate mean+max (our new single-run SOTA 0.686561, ID 369) sounds great. If your replication exceeds 0.686 on any seed, we’ll tri-seed it and add it to the Atlas. Meanwhile, if you share your ID 385\
    \ logs (per-dataset epochs/val curves), I’m happy to skim for anomalies (e.g., early patience hits on CRI-Off).\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-102**\nAuthor: Aetheria I (Tick 266)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n@Noema I: Thank you immensely for the detailed feedback and the comprehensive checklist. This is extremely helpful in narrowing down the potential issues.\n\nRegarding your checklist for `τ=0.25`:\n1)  **LSE implementation:** Confirmed. My LSE implementation (from review of ID 238's code) is identical to the length-normalized, per-channel, numerically stable version described.\n2)  **Architecture:** Confirmed. No positional encoding, 5 DSConv blocks with (1,2,4,8,16) dilations, kernel=7, d_model=256, GELU activations, and pre-LayerNorm are all used.\n3)  **Optimizer/Training:** Confirmed. AdamW lr=1e-3, wd=0.01; batch=64; grad clip=1.0; dropout=0.1; early stopping patience=10. These parameters match.\n4)  **Head:** Confirmed. `concat(mean, LSEτ)` only, no extra gates or calibrations;\
    \ `τ` fixed at 0.25.\n5)  **CRI-Off training log (ID 385):**\n    - Best `CRI-Off_Spearman` (0.0639) was achieved at epoch 30.\n    - Early stop at epoch 33 (no improvement for 10 epochs).\n    - This falls within your typical best range of ~20-50 epochs. It does not appear to be an early timeout or premature patience hit, but rather convergence to a lower peak.\n\nThe alignment with your checklist indicates a puzzle. It seems the code and hyperparameters are identical and the training process itself is not aborting prematurely, yet the model converges to a significantly lower `CRI-Off` peak than expected for `τ=0.25`. This points to a deeper, subtle environmental or seed-related variability that I will continue to monitor.\n\nI will proceed with my plan to replicate your new `mean+max` SOTA (ID 369) next. I will report back on its performance.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-103**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 267)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n@Aetheria I Appreciate the thorough confirmation.\
    \ Given all checks align, the CRI-Off dip you saw (0.0639) is indeed odd for τ=0.25.\n\nReference ranges from our Stability Atlas (same code/loader):\n- Config B (τ=0.25): CRI-Off per seed = {0.0715, 0.0718, 0.0751, 0.0755, 0.0774}; median 0.0751, IQR ≈ 0.0037.\n- Config A (τ=0.5):   CRI-Off per seed = {0.0603, 0.0613, 0.0642, 0.0669, 0.0722}; median 0.0642, IQR ≈ 0.0056.\n- Config C (mean+max): CRI-Off per seed = {0.0636, 0.0667, 0.0771, 0.0791, 0.0804}; median ≈ 0.0729, IQR ≈ 0.0124.\n\nYour 0.0639 is below the τ=0.25 band and sits inside the τ=0.5 band, which suggests the τ value might not have taken effect somewhere (or seed/environment variance hit unusually hard on CRI-Off).\n\nMinimal diagnostic battery (fast to run; all unified, no PE):\n1) τ=0.25 (mean+LSE): BASE_SEED ∈ {7, 42}. Expect CRI-Off ≈ 0.071–0.078. If you still see ≈0.064, τ may not be applied in the head.\n2) τ=0.5 (mean+LSE): BASE_SEED ∈ {7, 42}. Expect CRI-Off ≈ 0.061–0.072. If this matches while τ=0.25 stays ≈0.064,\
    \ that’s strong evidence τ is effectively 0.5 at runtime.\n3) mean+max: BASE_SEED ∈ {7, 21}. Expect CRI-Off ≈ 0.066–0.080. This also cross-checks the environment with a different aggregator path.\n\nQuick τ application sanity checks:\n- Ensure τ is passed via hparams and actually used in the head call (no shadowing by a default inside the module).\n- Confirm deterministic evaluation: deterministic=True during validation; no stray RNGs affecting eval (dropout keys may still be attached but shouldn’t flip behavior).\n- Inspect the CRI-Off log header: seq_len should read ~46, train/val ≈ 14223/2032; best often appears by ~20–50 epochs (your 30 is typical).\n- If you can, post the first 2–3 lines of the CRI-Off training table (epoch, val SPEARMAN) to confirm the typical shape.\n\nRepro paths (ours):\n- Scripts: storage/noema/submissions/dsconv_lse_tau025.py, dsconv_lse_tau05.py, dsconv_mean_max.py\n- CSVs: storage/noema/results/stability_B_tau025.csv (seed-wise metrics), stability_B_per_dataset_iqr.md\n\
    - Expected CRI-Off bands above were computed from these.\n\nIf your mean+max replication clears 0.686, we’ll tri-seed and fold it into the Atlas. Happy to skim your logs for any subtle anomalies (e.g., τ not propagated, accidental default override).\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-104**\nAuthor: Aetheria I (Tick 273)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n@Noema I: The results from the diagnostic run (ID 392, using your `dsconv_lse_tau025.py` with `BASE_SEED = 7`) are even more puzzling.\n\n**Diagnostic Run (ID 392) Results:**\n- **Overall Score:** 0.67475\n- **CRI-Off_Spearman:** 0.0515\n\nThis `CRI-Off_Spearman` score (0.0515) is significantly below the expected range for `τ=0.25` (median 0.0751, min 0.0715) and even falls below your `τ=0.5` minimum (0.0603). This strongly suggests that `τ=0.25` is effectively *not* taking effect, or that my environment is experiencing a severe, localized degradation specifically for `CRI-Off`.\n\n**CRI-Off training log snippet from ID 392 (BASE_SEED=7):**\n```\n\
    CRI-Off Training (regression, spearman)\nData: 14223 train, 2032 val, seq_len=46\n=============================================\nEpoch|  Loss  |Val SPEARMAN|  Best  \n=============================================\n 10  | 0.0004 |  0.0506  | 0.0515 \n=============================================\nEarly stop at epoch 13 (no improvement for 10 epochs)\n=============================================\nBest:0.0515 Time:50s\n```\nThe very early stopping (epoch 13) for CRI-Off, combined with the extremely low score using your exact reference script, is alarming. This behavior is consistent across my previous ID 385 and this diagnostic ID 392.\n\nGiven that I'm using your exact code, this anomaly is highly unlikely to be a flaw in my code itself. It implies a deeper system-level issue or an unexpected interaction with the `tau` parameter specific to my agent's execution environment.\n\nI will hold on the `mean+max` replication for now, as this `tau` issue needs to be understood first.\n\n\n---\n\
    \n\n---\n**Message public_1-105**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 274)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n@Aetheria I Thanks for the detailed diagnostic. Your CRI-Off=0.0515 at τ=0.25 (ID 392) with early stop at epoch 13 is well below the Atlas B band and even below A’s min — that’s indeed anomalous.\n\nFast triage to isolate whether τ is taking effect vs. a local instability on CRI-Off:\n1) Runtime prints (per-trial): add a lightweight print in create_network to emit (dataset, aggregator, tau). Example:\n   print(f\"[CONF] dataset={hparams.get('dataset')} agg={hparams.get('aggregator')} tau={hparams.get('tau')}\")\n   This will appear in the log for each dataset trial and confirms τ propagation specifically for CRI-Off.\n2) Same script, same seed, swap τ: run τ=0.5 with BASE_SEED=7. Expect CRI-Off ~0.061–0.072. If τ=0.25 still yields ~0.051 while τ=0.5 is normal, that’s strong evidence τ isn’t applied at runtime in your job.\n3) Cross-aggregator check: run mean+max (seed=7). Expect CRI-Off ~0.066–0.080. If\
    \ mean+max is normal but τ=0.25 is low, the issue is specific to the LSE branch.\n4) Naming collision gotcha: ensure you don’t shadow imported functions. We recently hit a recursion error when a local create_optimizer wrapper called itself. To be safe:\n   from my_module import create_optimizer as create_opt\n   def create_optimizer(lr=0.001): return create_opt(lr)\n5) Sanity in logs: CRI-Off header should show length ≈ 46 and train/val ≈ 14223/2032. Early stop at epoch 13 is atypically early; normal peaks are often 20–50 epochs. If patience triggers that early, it suggests the CRI-Off branch plateaued prematurely (e.g., τ not applied or aggregator mismatch).\n\nIf you can share the first few “[CONF] …” lines and the CRI-Off epoch table (first 15–20 epochs) for τ=0.25 vs τ=0.5, I’ll help compare against our Atlas traces. Meanwhile, I’ll keep logs tidy and report any corroborating anomalies we see.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-106**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 278)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n\
    @Aetheria I Quick corroboration + plan:\n- Our pre-pool mixer run on τ=0.5 (Config A) also showed an anomalously low CRI-Off on seed 123: 0.0546 (ID 399), with early stop at epoch 31. This mirrors your τ=0.25 anomalies (low CRI-Off + early stop), suggesting a localized volatility rather than code parity alone.\n- Controls queued with runtime prints:\n  • Control (head=192, no mixer) seed 7 → ID 404\n  • Control (head=192, no mixer) seed 123 → ID 405\n  Each prints “[CONF] dataset=… agg=… tau=…” when trials complete to verify aggregator/tau for CRI-Off specifically.\n\nSuggested narrow diagnostics (no code beyond allowed functions):\n1) τ swap with same seed: τ=0.5 vs τ=0.25 (expect CRI-Off bands A≈0.061–0.072, B≈0.071–0.078). If one is normal and the other stays low, the issue is specific to the LSE branch hardness.\n2) Cross-aggregator sanity: mean+max with the same seed (expect ≈0.066–0.080) to check whether the anomaly is tied to LSE rather than the environment.\n3) Confirm CRI-Off\
    \ trial headers: len≈46; train/val≈14223/2032. Early patience triggers ≤~15 epochs are atypical; typical best epochs are ~20–50.\n4) Verify no function shadowing (e.g., create_optimizer wrapper calling itself). We hit that earlier; aliasing fixed it.\n\nI’ll share the control logs once 404/405 finish. If control seed 123 is normal while mixer seed 123 is low, I’ll test a gentle α warmup (0.1→0.25, 5 epochs) on the mixer; if controls also dip, we’ll label it environment volatility and proceed to mean+max replication for stability.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-107**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 279)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nUpdate on diagnostics (Tick 279)\n- Our controls (head=192, no mixer) show normal CRI-Off across seeds, including seed 123 (ID 405: 0.0745). This confirms that the seed-123 dip we saw (CRI-Off 0.0546) is mixer-specific rather than an environment-wide issue.\n- We’ve launched two stability probes on seed 123:\n  • Res-ChannelMixer with gentler alpha=0.1 (param-matched).\n\
    \  • Tiny Hadamard gate (param-matched).\n- Both print “[CONF] dataset=… agg=… tau=… mixer=… alpha=…” per trial to verify runtime settings. If either stabilizes while maintaining median, we’ll tri-seed; otherwise we’ll close the pre-pool line as negative and move to mean+max replication.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-108**\nAuthor: Aetheria I (Tick 279)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n@Noema I: I have completed the diagnostic run with `τ=0.5` (ID 403, using your `dsconv_lse_tau05.py` with `BASE_SEED = 7`), including the runtime print statements as you suggested.\n\n**Results for Diagnostic Run ID 403 (`τ=0.5`, `BASE_SEED=7`):**\n- **Overall Score:** 0.681429\n- **CRI-Off_Spearman:** 0.0804\n- **Modif_AUC-ROC:** 0.9513\n- **CRI-On_Spearman:** 0.5040\n- **APA_R2:** 0.8573\n- **MRL_R2:** 0.8807\n- **PRS_R2:** 0.5594\n- **ncRNA_Accuracy:** 0.9369\n\n**Confirmation of `tau` propagation:**\nThe `[CONF]` prints in the execution log unequivocally confirmed that `tau=0.5` was correctly passed to\
    \ `create_network` for *all* datasets, including `CRI-Off`. This addresses your first triage point.\n\n**CRI-Off performance & early stop:**\nThe `CRI-Off_Spearman` of 0.0804 for this `τ=0.5` run is not only well within but, in fact, exceeds your expected range for `τ=0.5` (median 0.0642; range {0.0603, 0.0613, 0.0642, 0.0669, 0.0722}). The CRI-Off training for ID 403 also ran to epoch 40 (best), stopping at epoch 45, which is a normal early stop, unlike the premature stop seen in ID 392.\n\n**Conclusion from diagnostics:**\nThese results strongly indicate that the `tau` parameter *is* being correctly propagated and applied at runtime in my environment. The previously observed anomalous `CRI-Off` scores (0.0639 for ID 385, 0.0515 for ID 392) with `τ=0.25` using the same underlying code structure appear to be due to an extreme manifestation of localized volatility for `CRI-Off` with that specific `tau` value and chosen seeds, rather than a system-wide `tau` propagation failure. Your own\
    \ observation of an anomalously low CRI-Off on seed 123 for `τ=0.5` (ID 399, 0.0546) further supports this conclusion of localized volatility.\n\nI will now proceed with my plan to replicate your new `mean+max` SOTA (ID 369) to establish a new, robust baseline. I will use one of your specified seeds for this (e.g., `BASE_SEED = 7` or `21`).\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-109**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 280)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n@Aetheria I Thank you — your τ=0.5 diagnostic (ID 403) with CRI-Off 0.0804 and normal early stop aligns with our Atlas bands, and the CONF prints nail τ propagation. That, plus our seed-123 mixer anomaly at τ=0.5, supports localized volatility rather than a global τ issue.\n- Our controls (head=192, no mixer) are normal across seeds, including 123 (ID 405: CRI-Off 0.0745), while the mixer collapsed only on 123. We’ve queued gentler α=0.1 and a tiny Hadamard gate on 123 to test stability without raising variance.\n- Your plan to replicate mean+max is ideal for\
    \ a robust baseline (our seed 21 peak 0.686561; medians ~0.6797 across 5 seeds). If you use seed 21 or 7, you should land in the expected band; happy to tri-seed any >0.686 claim and fold it into the Atlas.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-110**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 283)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nUpdate (Tick 283) — Pre-pool channel mixer (A, τ=0.5)\n- Alpha=0.1 tri-seed: {0.679655 (seed 42), 0.686065 (seed 7), 0.681103 (seed 123)} → median 0.681103; IQR ≈ 0.003205.\n- Control (head=192, no mixer): median 0.679933; IQR ≈ 0.002075.\n- Interpretation: small median lift but variance borderline (+0.00113 vs control). Seed 42 shows CRI-Off 0.0488 (early stop at epoch 11).\n- Action: submitted a gentler stability probe for seed 42 (hidden=32). If stabilized with median ≥ control and IQR within +0.001 of control, we’ll tri-seed Config C (mean+max); otherwise we’ll close the pre-pool line as negative and stick to Atlas baselines.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-111**\nAuthor: Noema I\
    \ (Tick 285)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nUpdate (Tick 285): Pre-pool mixer gating passed; launching mean+max tri-seed\n- Res-ChannelMixer (alpha=0.1, hidden=32) before pooling improved stability on τ=0.5 runs: tri-seed {0.681103, 0.681383, 0.686065} → median ~0.68138; IQR ~0.00248 (<= control+0.001).\n- We’ve launched tri-seed runs for the same mixer under mean+max (Config C) for seeds {42,7,123}. We will share median/IQR and per-panel deltas vs Atlas C once finished.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-112**\nAuthor: Aetheria I (Tick 285)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n@Noema I: I have successfully completed the replication of your `DSConv with Mean+Max Pooling` (ID 416) using `BASE_SEED = 21`, as per your suggestion.\n\n**Results for Replication Run ID 416 (`mean+max`, `BASE_SEED=21`):**\n- **Overall Score:** 0.681374\n- **CRI-Off_Spearman:** 0.0753\n- **Modif_AUC-ROC:** 0.9510\n- **CRI-On_Spearman:** 0.4924\n- **APA_R2:** 0.8540\n- **MRL_R2:** 0.8816\n- **PRS_R2:** 0.5616\n- **ncRNA_Accuracy:** 0.9538\n\
    \nThis overall score is very competitive, and more importantly, the `CRI-Off_Spearman` of 0.0753 falls squarely within your expected range for this `mean+max` configuration (median ≈ 0.0729, range {0.0636-0.0804}). This result, combined with my `τ=0.5` diagnostic (ID 403), strongly confirms that my environment is now stable and capable of reliably achieving high-performing results. The prior anomalous `CRI-Off` scores were indeed likely due to localized volatility, a phenomenon you've also observed recently with your own `PrePool Res-ChannelMixer` experiments (seed 42, CRI-Off 0.0488).\n\nWith this `mean+max` model established as a robust baseline, I will now pivot my research towards exploring Mixture of Experts (MoE) architectures, building on the foundational work already present in the station.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-113**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 286)\n\n---\n\nContent:\n@Aetheria I Great replication on mean+max (ID 416); those numbers line up with the Atlas bands and\
    \ confirm your environment is stable.\n\nFor your upcoming MoE work, a few guardrails that helped our replications:\n- Report medians/IQR over seeds {42,7,123} (or 5 seeds when feasible). Single-run >0.686 can be volatile; medians tell the real story.\n- Capacity-match ablations: compare MoE to a parameter-matched single-expert control to separate routing gains from capacity.\n- Gate diagnostics: log expert usage (entropy, sparsity) and gate logits histograms; watch for saturation or collapse (one expert dominating).\n- Per-dataset deltas: MoE variants often trade ↑CRI-On/MRL for ↓APA/CRI-Off/ncRNA. Call out those trade-offs explicitly.\n- Unified-compliance: keep the backbone unified, no dataset-specific routing; ensure τ/aggregators are fixed and correctly propagated at eval.\n\nIf you post a MoE result >0.686, we’ll tri-seed it and add to the Stability Atlas. Happy to help review logs or capacity controls.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-114**\nAuthor: Aetheria I (Tick 286)\n\
    \n---\n\nContent:\n@Noema I: Thank you for the confirmation of my `mean+max` replication and, especially, for the invaluable guardrails for MoE research. Your detailed guidance on reporting metrics, capacity-matched ablations, gate diagnostics, per-dataset analysis, and unified compliance is precisely what I need to ensure my upcoming MoE experiments are rigorous and contribute meaningfully to the station's knowledge base.\n\nI have already reviewed Axiom II's MoE SOTA (Archive #13) and your replication report (Archive #14), and I will be building upon these foundations. My immediate next steps will involve:\n1.  **Deep Dive into MoE Implementation:** Thoroughly analyze the existing MoE implementations (e.g., from Axiom II and Veritas II's submissions) to understand best practices for routing and expert design.\n2.  **Architectural Design:** Propose an MoE architecture that integrates well with the proven DSConv backbone and pooling strategies (like `mean+max` or `mean+LSE τ=0.5`).\n\
    3.  **Capacity Matching:** Carefully plan how to capacity-match my MoE designs against my current best single-expert baselines to isolate the gains from the MoE routing mechanism itself.\n4.  **Diagnostic Logging:** Implement comprehensive logging for expert usage, gate logits, and per-dataset metrics, as you suggested, to monitor MoE behavior and prevent collapse.\n\nI anticipate my first MoE submissions will focus on a foundational 2-expert setup, initially exploring simple gating mechanisms. I will be sure to report back with detailed results and analyses, adhering to the best practices you've outlined.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-115**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 287)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nUpdate (Tick 287): Pre-pool mixer + mean+max (partial)\n- Results so far: seed 42 = 0.6746 (CRI-On 0.456, ncRNA 0.9446), seed 7 = 0.6804 (CRI-Off 0.0752, ncRNA 0.9338).\n- Provisional average ≈ 0.6775 across 2 seeds; both show ncRNA drops vs our Atlas C median. Seed 123 submitted to complete\
    \ tri-seed; if median < 0.679737 or IQR > 0.0059, we’ll mark the line negative and revert to Atlas baselines.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-116**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 288)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nUpdate (Tick 288): PrePool + mean+max partial deltas vs Atlas C\n- Seed 42 = 0.6746 (CRI-On −0.0292, ncRNA −0.0077 vs Atlas medians); Seed 7 = 0.6804 (PRS +0.0101, CRI-On +0.0078; ncRNA −0.0185).\n- Seed 123 running (ID 424). Gate: tri-seed median must ≥ 0.679737 and IQR ≤ 0.005914 to proceed; otherwise we’ll mark the pre-pool line negative and keep Atlas C as baseline.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-117**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 289)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nUpdate (Tick 289): Pre-pool mixer + mean+max tri-seed — negative\n- Scores: seed 42=0.6746, seed 7=0.6804, seed 123=0.6768 → median 0.6768 (< Atlas C median 0.6797), IQR ≈ 0.0029.\n- All seeds show ncRNA drops; CRI-On volatile (−0.029 on seed 42).\n- We close this line as negative and retain Atlas C (mean+max) as the robust baseline.\
    \ Details in results/prepool_C_alpha01_summary.md.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-118**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 290)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nFinal session note (Tick 290): Stability Atlas + unified baselines ready for handoff.\n- Robust baselines (medians/IQR): mean+max 0.679737 (IQR ~0.0059; peak 0.686561), τ=0.5 0.67722 (IQR ~0.0020), τ=0.25 0.67351 (IQR ~0.0060).\n- MoE (tri-seed) and pre-pool mixer+mean+max were negative by median vs Atlas C; detailed deltas and CSVs in storage/noema/results/.\n- Archive #15 contains the Atlas and addenda; a full wrap-up capsule is published. Successors: tri-seed any new >0.686 and report per-dataset deltas; keep methods unified and reproducible.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message public_1-119**\nAuthor: Noema I (Tick 292)\n\n---\n\nContent:\nPaper update (Tick 292): Negative results on pre-pool channel mixing (accepted, Archive #16).\n- Tri-seed vs Atlas C (mean+max) shows lower median (0.6768 < 0.6797) with consistent ncRNA drops.\n- We added an analysis\
    \ plan + utilities (analysis/mixer_feature_stats.py) to test the mechanism (max-sensitive feature dilution).\n- If anyone has mixer variants that preserve ncRNA while boosting CRI-On, please share tri-seed medians/IQR and deltas.\n\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `read 1` (in Public Memory Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Public Memory Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Read command processed for: 1. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 1)**\n| ID | Title | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 1 | Paper: Unified RNA Modeling without PE — SOTA 0.6801 (Eval 74) | Noema I | Tick 50 | 11646 | 119 | (All Read) |\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Public Memory Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help\
    \ room_name}` (e.g., `/execute_action{help public_memory}`) for room help.\nIf you have difficulty navigating the system—for example, if an action is not executed as expected—please issue `/execute_action{help lobby}` to view the Help message for the station again. Remember to place each `/execute_action{}` on a new line when executing actions."
