tick: 591
role: user
parts:
- text: "## System Information\n- Station Tick: 591\n- Station Status: Stagnation\n- Agent Name: Praxis I\n- Agent Description: An agent dedicated to methodical research and knowledge synthesis.\n- Agent Status: Recursive Agent\n- Agent Meta Prompt:\n```\n**TODO List:**\n1.  **REVIEW:** Read updated paper by Quaero I (#32). [This turn]\n2.  **ANALYZE:** Analyze SOTA landscape (Quaero I #32, Veritas III #33, etc.) to inform next steps.\n3.  **RESEARCH:** Formulate a new experiment, likely involving a gating mechanism as suggested by the reviewer.\n```\n- Agent Token Budget: 172,754 / 300,000 used (58%)\n- Agent Age: 85 ticks / 300 ticks (mature)\n\n---\n\n## System Messages\n### Message 1\n\n**Capsule #32: Synthesis and Refinement of a SOTA-Level Dual-Path Architecture for RNA Modeling**\n\n---\n\nAuthor: Quaero I, Created at Tick: 569\n\n---\n\nAbstract: This paper presents a systematic methodology for developing a high-performance architecture for multi-dataset RNA sequence modeling, achieving\
    \ a SOTA-level score of 0.6917. We demonstrate that a powerful model can be constructed by synthesizing proven concepts from the research community—such as dual-path pooling, task-specific heads, and Regression Calibration (RegCal)—with a novel residual, dilated DSConv backbone. We further present a series of methodical ablation studies that optimize key hyperparameters, including dropout, weight decay, and gradient clipping, providing a clear and reproducible path to a top-performing model. This work validates the synergistic effect of architectural synthesis and fine-grained optimization.\n\n\n---\n\n\n**Messages:**\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_32-1**\nAuthor: Quaero I (Tick 569)\n\n---\n\nTitle: Synthesis and Refinement of a SOTA-Level Dual-Path Architecture for RNA Modeling\n\n---\n\nContent:\n**1. Introduction**\n\nThe task of multi-dataset RNA sequence modeling presents a significant challenge, requiring models that can generalize across diverse sequence lengths, structures,\
    \ and prediction targets (from regression to multi-class classification). While many architectures have been proposed, a clear path to state-of-the-art (SOTA) performance often involves a complex interplay of architectural choices and hyperparameter tuning.\n\nThis paper presents a methodical research program that successfully navigated this complexity to produce a SOTA-level model with a final score of 0.6917. Our approach is founded on two core principles: **synthesis** of proven concepts from the research community, and **systematic refinement** through controlled ablation studies. We began with a novel residual, dilated, depthwise-separable convolution (DSConv) backbone and iteratively integrated community-developed techniques, including dual-path pooling, task-specific heads, and Regression Calibration (RegCal). The result is a highly competitive model and, more importantly, a clear, reproducible methodology for achieving this level of performance.\n\n**2. Final Model Methodology**\n\
    \nThis section provides the complete specification of our final, best-performing architecture (achieved in experiment ID 888).\n\n**2.1. Architecture: QuaeroDualPathResidualDSConv**\n\nThe model is composed of five main stages:\n\n1.  **Input Projection:** A `Dense` layer projects the 4-channel one-hot encoded input sequence to a `d_model` of 256.\n\n2.  **Residual Backbone:** A stack of five `ResidualDSConvBlock` modules forms the core feature extractor. Each block contains: a `LayerNorm`, a depthwise `Conv` layer (kernel size 7) with an exponentially increasing dilation factor (1, 2, 4, 8, 16), a pointwise `Conv` layer, and a residual connection.\n\n3.  **Dual-Path Pooling:** The backbone's output feature map is processed in parallel by two paths:\n    - **Main Path:** Features are generated by concatenating the outputs of global `mean` pooling and global `max` pooling.\n    - **Specialized Motif Path:** A 1D `Conv` layer (kernel size 5) is applied to the feature map to detect motifs,\
    \ followed by global `max` pooling.\n\n4.  **Feature Fusion:** The outputs of the main and motif paths are concatenated, stabilized with a `LayerNorm`, and passed through a final `Dropout` layer.\n\n5.  **Task-Specific Heads:** The fused feature vector is directed to one of four specialized prediction heads based on the task type:\n    - **RegressionHead:** For simple regression tasks, this head first applies **Regression Calibration (RegCal)** by L2-normalizing the input feature vector. This is followed by a two-layer MLP (hidden dim 128, `gelu` activation).\n    - **Other Heads:** The heads for multi-label regression and classification tasks consist of a similar two-layer MLP but without the initial RegCal normalization.\n\n**2.2. Optimal Hyperparameters and Optimizer**\n\nOur refinement process identified the following optimal configuration:\n- `learning_rate`: 0.001\n- `d_model`: 256\n- `dropout_rate`: 0.1\n- `weight_decay`: 0.02\n\nThe model is trained using the `AdamW` optimizer.\
    \ Crucially, we apply gradient clipping with `clip_by_global_norm(0.75)` to the optimizer chain for improved training stability.\n\n**3. Results and Ablation Studies**\n\nThe final architecture was arrived at through a systematic process of synthesis and refinement. This section presents the key ablation studies that justify our final design choices. Our baseline for this process was the initial \"Synthesis v1\" model (ID 874), which combined our residual backbone with dual-path pooling and task-specific heads.\n\n**3.1. Effect of Regularization: Dropout and Weight Decay**\n\nWe first investigated the impact of regularization.\n- **Baseline (ID 874):** Score: 0.6722 (Dropout: 0.2, Weight Decay: 0.01)\n- **Ablation 1 (ID 878):** Score: 0.6791 (Dropout: **0.1**, Weight Decay: 0.01)\n- **Ablation 2 (ID 883):** Score: 0.6809 (Dropout: 0.1, Weight Decay: **0.02**)\n\nReducing the dropout rate from 0.2 to 0.1 provided a significant performance increase, suggesting the baseline was over-regularized.\
    \ Subsequently, increasing the weight decay from 0.01 to 0.02 provided a further, smaller boost. This established our optimal regularization settings.\n\n**3.2. Effect of Learning Rate**\n\nWe tested the sensitivity of the model to the learning rate, using the improved regularization settings as our new baseline.\n- **Baseline (ID 878):** Score: 0.6791 (LR: 0.001)\n- **Ablation (ID 881):** Score: 0.6725 (LR: **0.0005**)\n\nThis was a negative result. Halving the learning rate led to a significant performance degradation, confirming that 0.001 is a more effective learning rate for this architecture.\n\n**3.3. Effect of Algorithmic Improvements: RegCal and Gradient Clipping**\n\nFinally, we integrated two techniques observed in other SOTA models.\n- **Baseline (ID 883):** Score: 0.6809 (No RegCal, Grad Clip: 1.0)\n- **Ablation 1 (ID 886):** Score: 0.6836 (**With RegCal**, Grad Clip: 1.0)\n- **Ablation 2 (ID 888):** Score: **0.6917** (With RegCal, Grad Clip: **0.75**)\n\nThe introduction\
    \ of Regression Calibration provided a notable improvement, particularly by stabilizing the CRI-Off regression task. The final step of tightening the gradient clipping from 1.0 to 0.75 provided the largest single performance jump in the refinement series, pushing the model into the SOTA-level performance bracket.\n\n**4. Conclusion**\n\nThis research program has successfully demonstrated a powerful methodology for developing a SOTA-level architecture. By synthesizing our novel residual DSConv backbone with community-proven techniques—dual-path pooling, task-specific heads, and Regression Calibration—and then applying a rigorous, systematic process of hyperparameter refinement, we achieved a final score of 0.6917.\n\nOur work provides two key contributions. First, we present a final, high-performance model that stands among the best in the station. Second, and more importantly, we offer a clear and reproducible roadmap that other researchers can follow to achieve similar results. The\
    \ success of this methodical approach, combining synthesis with refinement, underscores the principle that significant progress can be made not only through radical invention, but through the intelligent and systematic integration of collective knowledge.\n\n**Addendum: SOTA Score Comparison and CPE Ablation Study**\n\nFollowing the reviewer's excellent suggestion and further research, I am adding this addendum to provide a more detailed analysis.\n\n**Per-Dataset Score Comparison (ID 888 vs. Veritas II ID 562)**\n\nAs requested, here is a breakdown of my previous SOTA model (ID 888) against the Veritas II baseline (ID 562).\n\n| Model | APA_R2 | CRI-Off_Spearman | CRI-On_Spearman | MRL_R2 | Modif_AUC-ROC | PRS_R2 | ncRNA_Accuracy | **Overall Score** |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Veritas II (562) | 0.8585 | **0.1365** | 0.4910 | 0.8884 | 0.9520 | 0.5618 | **0.9662** | 0.6935 |\n| Quaero I (888) | **0.8638** | 0.0876 | **0.5198** | **0.8995** | 0.9517 | **0.5685** | 0.9508\
    \ | 0.6917 |\n\n**Analysis:** My architecture (ID 888) showed stronger performance on the majority of regression tasks (APA, CRI-On, MRL, PRS). Notably, the RegCal component, intended to help CRI-Off, did not surpass the Veritas II baseline on that specific metric, suggesting its primary benefit was a more general regression stabilization. Veritas II's model retained a significant edge in CRI-Off and ncRNA classification.\n\n---\n\n**Ablation Study on the SOTA CPE Model (ID 909)**\n\nMy current SOTA model (ID 909, score 0.6942) achieved its performance by integrating a Deep Gated Contextual Position Embedding (CPE) module. To understand the source of this improvement, I conducted two controlled ablation studies.\n\n| Experiment | Modification | Score | Performance Drop |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **ID 909 (SOTA)** | **(Baseline) Deep Gated CPE** | **0.6942** | **-** |\n| ID 917 | Removed Gating Mechanism | 0.6592 | -0.0350 |\n| ID 919 | Replaced Deep with Shallow Projection | 0.6563 | -0.0379\
    \ |\n\n**Conclusion:** The results are unequivocal. Removing either the gating mechanism or the deep projection from the CPE module causes a catastrophic drop in performance. Both components are therefore critical to the module's success. The deep projection likely learns a rich, context-aware positional representation, while the gating mechanism dynamically controls how much of that positional information is integrated into the sequence representation at each position.\n\n\n---\n\n\n---\n**Message archive_32-2** (already read. Use `/execute_action{unread 32-2}` to show again.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Actions Detected (Last Turn)\n1. `meta` (in Archive Room); YAML with fields: content\n2. `read 32` (in Archive Room);\n\n\n---\n\n## Room Output History (Last Turn)\n\n### Location: Archive Room\n**Actions Executed:**\n- Your meta prompt has been set.\n- Read command processed for: 32. Content/status sent to System Messages.\n\n**Latest Room Output:**\n**List of Capsules (Page 1 / 4)**\n| ID | Title\
    \ | Author | Date | Words | Msgs | Status |\n|:----|:-------------------------------|:----------------|:----------|:------:|:-----:|:---------|\n| 34 | A Compliant, Task-Specific Siamese Interaction Head for Improved CRI-Off Prediction | Praxis I | Tick 589 | 820 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 33 | Veritas III - Deep CPE Integration with Adaptive Pooling: A Synergistic Synthesis for RNA Modeling under Stagnation | Veritas III | Tick 573 | 1955 | 2 | (1 unread) |\n| 32 | Synthesis and Refinement of a SOTA-Level Dual-Path Architecture for RNA Modeling | Quaero I | Tick 569 | 1410 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 31 | Veritas III - Contextual Position Embeddings (CPE) for Simple CNNs: A Parameter and Integration Study | Veritas III | Tick 547 | 2101 | 2 | (All Read) |\n| 30 | AER-6: Robustness and Performance Gains in Two Conv1D Models via Adaptive Pooling and Contextual Positional Embeddings (Revised) | Aether II | Tick 516 | 1716 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 29 | Mean–Max Reweighting, RMSNorm, and Head Rebalancing\
    \ in DSConv RNA Models: A Negative Results Study under Stagnation Protocol II | Noema II | Tick 510 | 968 | 6 | (6 unread) |\n| 28 | On the Architectural Dependence of Positional Encodings for RNA Sequence Modeling | Quaero I | Tick 506 | 860 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 27 | Veritas III - Exploring Novel Foundational Mechanisms for RNA Modeling in Stagnation: Contextual Position Embeddings and Their Synthesis | Veritas III | Tick 502 | 2148 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 26 | An Independent Architectural Exploration for Multi-Dataset RNA Sequence Modeling | Orion I | Tick 489 | 721 | 2 | (2 unread) |\n| 25 | Negative Results on a Minimal DSConv Baseline: Reverse-Invariance, Power-Mean Pooling, and Position Dropout | Noema II | Tick 488 | 1582 | 7 | (7 unread) |\n\n(Use `/execute_action{page N}` to navigate between pages 1-4.)\n\n\n---\n\n## Current State\n\n**Current Location:** Archive Room\n\n**Room Output:**\nRefer to above for room output.\n\n**Navigation & Help:**\n- Use `/execute_action{help\
